{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malavika","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Malavika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahaf","familyName":"Aljundi","name":"Aljundi, Rahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikechukwu","familyName":"Nkisi-Orji","name":"Nkisi-Orji, Ikechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalie","familyName":"Wiratunga","name":"Wiratunga, Nirmalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T14:37:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T13:07:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-19T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the large-scale adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) in various applications, there is a growing reliability concern due to their tendency to generate inaccurate text, i.e. hallucinations. In this work, we propose Cross-Layer Attention Probing (CLAP), a novel activation probing technique for hallucination detection, which processes the LLM activations across the entire residual stream as a joint sequence. Our empirical evaluations using five LLMs and three tasks show that CLAP improves hallucination detection compared to baselines on both greedy decoded responses as well as responses sampled at higher temperatures, thus enabling fine-grained detection, i.e. the ability to disambiguate hallucinations and non-hallucinations among different sampled responses to a given prompt. This allows us to propose a detect-then-mitigate strategy using CLAP to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM reliability compared to direct mitigation approaches. Finally, we show that CLAP maintains high reliability even when applied out-of-distribution. Code available at: https://github.com/itsmemala/CLAP"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the TRUST-AI workshop, ECAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiří","familyName":"Milička","name":"Milička, Jiří","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Marklová","name":"Marklová, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Václav","familyName":"Cvrček","name":"Cvrček, Václav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmark of stylistic variation in LLM-generated texts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:12:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T23:31:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:15:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1075/ijcl.25151.mil","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates the register variation in texts written by humans and comparable texts produced by large language models (LLMs). Biber's multidimensional analysis (MDA) is applied to a sample of human-written texts and AI-created texts generated to be their counterparts to find the dimensions of variation in which LLMs differ most significantly and most systematically from humans. As textual material, a new LLM-generated corpus AI-Brown is used, which is comparable to BE-21 (a Brown family corpus representing contemporary British English). Since all languages except English are underrepresented in the training data of frontier LLMs, similar analysis is replicated on Czech using AI-Koditex corpus and Czech multidimensional model. Examined were 16 frontier models in various settings and prompts, with emphasis placed on the difference between base models and instruction-tuned models. Based on this, a benchmark is created through which models can be compared with each other and ranked in interpretable dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Data and scripts: https://osf.io/hs7xt/. Interactive charts: https://www.korpus.cz/stylisticbenchmark/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-21T02:23:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr Golrokh","familyName":"Amin","name":"Amin, Sepehr Golrokh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devin","familyName":"Rhoads","name":"Rhoads, Devin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Fakhrmoosavi","name":"Fakhrmoosavi, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas E.","familyName":"Lownes","name":"Lownes, Nicholas E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John N.","familyName":"Ivan","name":"Ivan, John N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Individual Travel Diaries Using Large Language Models Informed by Census and Land-Use Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T17:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T23:59:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:18:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T15:21:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-16T01:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study introduces a Large Language Model (LLM) scheme for generating key attributes of travel diaries in agent-based transportation models, including purpose, mode and distance, to assess the underlying viability of LLMs for activity generation tasks. While traditional approaches rely on large quantities of proprietary household travel surveys, our method generates personas stochastically from open-source American Community Survey (ACS) and Smart Location Database (SLD) data, then synthesizes diaries through direct prompting. Our study features a novel one-to-cohort realism score: a composite of four metrics (Trip Count Score, Interval Score, Purpose Score, and Mode Score) validated against the Connecticut Statewide Transportation Study (CSTS) diaries, matched across demographic variables. Our validation utilizes Jensen-Shannon Divergence to measure distributional similarities between generated and real diaries. When compared to diaries generated with classical methods (Negative Binomial for trip generation; Multinomial Logit for mode/purpose) calibrated on the validation set, LLM generated diaries achieve comparable overall realism (LLM mean: 0.692 vs. 0.628). The LLM excels in determining trip purpose, and its trip mode predictions demonstrate greater consistency (a narrower Realism Score distribution). Meanwhile, classical models lead to better numerical estimates of trip count and activity duration. Aggregate validation confirms the LLM's statistical representativeness (LLM mean: 0.779 vs. 0.706), demonstrating LLM's zero-shot viability and establishing a quantifiable metric of diary realism for future synthetic diary evaluation systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Dongmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonah","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yoonah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eun-Ju","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Eun-Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human Psychometric Questionnaires Mischaracterize LLM Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:14:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T10:00:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-03T11:04:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-29T15:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:09:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-02T18:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We examine whether human psychometric questionnaires can serve as reliable tools for characterizing and predicting LLM behavior in everyday user interactions. We analyze eight open-source LLMs by comparing their value and personality profiles derived from two different methods: Likert self-reports on established questionnaires (PVQ-40/21 and BFI-44/10) and generation probabilities over value-laden responses to everyday user queries. The two profiles diverge substantially. Within-construct item consistency, often cited as evidence of stable LLM dispositions, disappears in generation probabilities. We find that established questionnaire items contain explicit lexical cues that allow models to recognize the target construct and respond in alignment-consistent, socially desirable ways, whereas realistic user queries contain far less recognizable cues. In addition, demographic persona prompts shift models' responses to human questionnaires in ways consistent with real human patterns, but no such shifts appear in the generation setting, highlighting that human questionnaires overestimate LLMs' ability to faithfully reproduce expected psychological traits when role-playing demographic personas. Overall, our study indicates that questionnaire scores alone should not be treated as evidence of LLMs' response tendencies in realistic user interactions, and supports generation-probability profiling with ecologically valid items as a complementary behavioral measure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:34Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:37:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jackson","familyName":"Eshbaugh","name":"Eshbaugh, Jackson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chetan","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Chetan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge","familyName":"Silveyra","name":"Silveyra, Jorge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synthetic Homes: A Multimodal Generative AI Pipeline for Residential Building Data Generation under Data Scarcity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T18:53:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T19:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:03:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-07T01:10:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:21:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-08T16:45:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-09T01:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-10T16:55:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-11T01:08:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-10T19:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:21:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.mlwa.2026.100959","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computational models have emerged as powerful tools for multi-scale energy modeling research at the building and urban scale, supporting data-driven analysis across building and urban energy systems. However, these models require large amounts of building parameter data that is often inaccessible, expensive to collect, or subject to privacy constraints. We introduce a modular framework that applies generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) to construct simulation-ready building datasets from publicly available records and imagery. To improve the reliability of this framework, we evaluate both its AI components and its overall result. Our occlusion analysis demonstrates that for our selected images, LLaVA achieves greater visual focus than a GPT-based alternative for building image processing. We also assess plausibility of our results against a national reference dataset, finding that our synthetic data overlaps more than 95% for three of the four selected variables. This work aims to reduce dependence on costly or restricted data sources, lowering barriers to building-scale energy research and Machine Learning (ML)-driven urban energy modeling, thereby providing simulation-ready datasets intended to support downstream applications such as energy modeling, retrofit analysis, and urban-scale simulation under data scarcity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages; 2 appendices; 6 figures; 4 tables. Code available at https://github.com/Lafayette-EshbaughSilveyra-Group/synthetic-homes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:19:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:19:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:14:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuokai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhuokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinglong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingze","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Benyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RecoWorld: Building Simulated Environments for Agentic Recommender Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:13:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-25T20:09:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present RecoWorld, a blueprint for building simulated environments tailored to agentic recommender systems. Such environments give agents a proper training space where they can learn from errors without impacting real users. RecoWorld distinguishes itself with a dual-view architecture: a simulated user and an agentic recommender engage in multi-turn interactions aimed at maximizing user retention. The user simulator reviews recommended items, updates its mindset, and when sensing potential user disengagement, generates reflective instructions. The agentic recommender adapts its recommendations by incorporating these user instructions and reasoning traces, creating a dynamic feedback loop that actively engages users. This process leverages the exceptional reasoning capabilities of modern LLMs. We explore diverse content representations within the simulator, including text-based, multimodal, and semantic ID modeling, and discuss how multi-turn RL enables the recommender to refine its strategies through iterative interactions. RecoWorld also supports multi-agent simulations, allowing creators to simulate the responses of targeted user populations. It marks an important first step toward recommender systems where users and agents collaboratively shape personalized information streams. We envision new interaction paradigms where \"user instructs, recommender responds,\" jointly optimizing user retention and engagement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"HCRS @ WWW 2026, Best Paper Award"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:02Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:47:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Jewitt","name":"Jewitt, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bram","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Bram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gopi Krishnan","familyName":"Rajbahadur","name":"Rajbahadur, Gopi Krishnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed E.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Ahmed E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Hugging Face to GitHub: Tracing License Drift in the Open-Source AI Ecosystem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:46:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:08:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T03:50:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:17:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hidden license conflicts in the open-source AI ecosystem pose serious legal and ethical risks, exposing organizations to potential litigation and users to undisclosed risk. However, the field lacks a data-driven understanding of how frequently these conflicts occur, where they originate, and which communities are most affected. We present the first end-to-end audit of licenses for datasets and models on Hugging Face, as well as their downstream integration into open-source software applications, covering 364 thousand datasets, 1.6 million models, and 140 thousand GitHub projects. Our empirical analysis reveals systemic non-compliance in which 35.5% of model-to-application transitions eliminate restrictive license clauses by relicensing under permissive terms. In addition, we prototype an extensible rule engine that encodes almost 200 SPDX and model-specific clauses for detecting license conflicts, which can solve 86.4% of license conflicts in software applications. To support future research, we release our dataset and the prototype engine. Our study highlights license compliance as a critical governance challenge in open-source AI and provides both the data and tools necessary to enable automated, AI-aware compliance at scale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables, pre-print"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:39Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:51:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kausik","familyName":"Hira","name":"Hira, Kausik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohd","familyName":"Zaki","name":"Zaki, Mohd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","name":"Mausam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N. M. Anoop","familyName":"Krishnan","name":"Krishnan, N. M. Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MatSKRAFT: A framework for large-scale materials knowledge extraction from scientific tables"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:55:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:47:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-05T21:14:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:49:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scientific progress increasingly depends on synthesizing knowledge across vast literature, yet most experimental data remains trapped in semi-structured formats that resist systematic extraction and analysis. Here, we present MatSKRAFT, a computational framework that automatically extracts and integrates materials science knowledge from tabular data at unprecedented scale. Our approach transforms tables into graph-based representations processed by constraint-driven GNNs that encode scientific principles directly into model architecture. MatSKRAFT significantly outperforms contemporary frontier large language models, achieving F1 scores of 89.33 for property extraction and 71.35 for composition extraction, while processing data 6-496 times faster compared to the fastest and the slowest models respectively, with modest hardware requirements. Applied to 66,267 tables from more than 45,500 research publications, we construct a comprehensive database containing 509,281 entries, including 104,000 compositions that expand coverage beyond major existing databases. This systematic approach reveals previously overlooked materials with distinct property combinations and enables data-driven discovery of composition-property relationships forming the cornerstone of materials and scientific discovery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:15Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Keyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yicun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeping","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangnan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Guangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfeng","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Hongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limited Reference, Reliable Generation: A Two-Component Framework for Tabular Data Generation in Low-Data Regimes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T04:34:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:15:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T13:10:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic tabular data generation is increasingly essential in machine learning, supporting downstream applications when real-world, high-quality tabular data is insufficient. Existing tabular generation approaches, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs) and fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs), typically require sufficient reference data, limiting their effectiveness in domain-specific datasets with scarce records. While prompt-based LLMs offer flexibility without parameter tuning, they often generate distributionally drifted data with localized redundancy, leading to degradation in downstream task performance. To overcome these issues, we propose ReFine, a framework that (i) extracts symbolic if-then rules from interpretable models and embeds them into prompts to explicitly guide the generation process toward the domain-specific distribution, and (ii) applies dual-granularity filtering that mitigates over-sampling patterns while preserving rare but informative samples to reduce localized redundancy. Extensive experiments on diverse benchmarks demonstrate that ReFine provides robust downstream utility, achieving a top-tier average rank across datasets and data regimes, with an average relative improvement of 7.48% in extreme low-data regimes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:42Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:16:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffAero: A GPU-Accelerated Differentiable Simulation Framework for Efficient Quadrotor Policy Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T13:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:33:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This letter introduces DiffAero, a lightweight, GPU-accelerated, and fully differentiable simulation framework designed for efficient quadrotor control policy learning. DiffAero supports both environment-level and agent-level parallelism and integrates multiple dynamics models, customizable sensor stacks (IMU, depth camera, and LiDAR), and diverse flight tasks within a unified, GPU-native training interface. By fully parallelizing both physics and rendering on the GPU, DiffAero eliminates CPU-GPU data transfer bottlenecks and delivers orders-of-magnitude improvements in simulation throughput. In contrast to existing simulators, DiffAero not only provides high-performance simulation but also serves as a research platform for exploring differentiable and hybrid learning algorithms. Extensive benchmarks and real-world flight experiments demonstrate that DiffAero and hybrid learning algorithms combined can learn robust flight policies in hours on consumer-grade hardware. The code is available at https://github.com/flyingbitac/diffaero."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 11 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:30:31Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:30:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:28:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keunwoo","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Keunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungheon","familyName":"Doh","name":"Doh, Seungheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhan","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Juhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TalkPlayData 2: An Agentic Synthetic Data Pipeline for Multimodal Conversational Music Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T05:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-27T17:44:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:43:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-05T18:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-08T20:52:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:09:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-02T15:04:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-03T01:05:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present TalkPlayData 2, a synthetic dataset for multimodal conversational music recommendation generated by an agentic data pipeline. In the proposed pipeline, multiple large language model (LLM) agents are created under various roles with specialized prompts and access to different parts of information, and the chat data is acquired by logging the conversation between the Listener LLM and the Recsys LLM. To cover various conversation scenarios, for each conversation, the Listener LLM is conditioned on a finetuned conversation goal. Finally, all the LLMs are multimodal with audio and images, allowing a simulation of multimodal recommendation and conversation. In the LLM-as-a-judge and subjective evaluation experiments, TalkPlayData 2 achieved the proposed goal in various aspects related to training a generative recommendation model for music. TalkPlayData 2 and its generation code are released at https://talkpl-ai.github.io."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:14Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:10:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai R.","familyName":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Kai R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roland M.","familyName":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Roland M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikko","familyName":"Rönkkö","name":"Rönkkö, Mikko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Starzl","name":"Starzl, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Edmondson","name":"Edmondson, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALIGNS: Unlocking nomological networks in psychological measurement through a large language model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; J.4; I.5.1; H.3.3; H.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T04:21:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T16:46:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:50:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-02T11:26:30Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; Error in algorithm explanation"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Psychological measurement is critical to many disciplines. Despite advances in measurement, building nomological networks, theoretical maps of how concepts and measures relate to establish validity, remains a challenge 70 years after Cronbach and Meehl proposed them as fundamental to validation. This limitation has practical consequences: clinical trials may fail to detect treatment effects, and public policy may target the wrong outcomes. We introduce Analysis of Latent Indicators to Generate Nomological Structures (ALIGNS), a large language model-based system trained with validated questionnaire measures. ALIGNS provides three comprehensive nomological networks containing over 550,000 indicators across psychology, medicine, social policy, and other fields. This represents the first application of large language models to solve a foundational problem in measurement validation. We report classification accuracy tests used to develop the model, as well as three evaluations. In the first evaluation, the widely used NIH PROMIS anxiety and depression instruments are shown to converge into a single dimension of emotional distress. The second evaluation examines child temperament measures and identifies four potential dimensions not captured by current frameworks, and questions one existing dimension. The third evaluation, an applicability check, engages expert psychometricians who assess the system's importance, accessibility, and suitability. ALIGNS is freely available at nomologicalnetwork.org, complementing traditional validation methods with large-scale nomological analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Error in algorithm explanation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:07Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hasibur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Hasibur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smit","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Smit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents' Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:08:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T01:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:15:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3790388","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) enable conversational agents (CAs) to express distinctive personalities, raising new questions about how such designs shape user perceptions. This study investigates how personality expression levels and user-agent personality alignment influence perceptions in goal-oriented tasks. In a between-subjects experiment (N=150), participants completed travel planning with CAs exhibiting low, medium, or high expression across the Big Five traits, controlled via our novel Trait Modulation Keys framework. Results revealed an inverted-U relationship: medium expression produced the most positive evaluations across Intelligence, Enjoyment, Anthropomorphism, Intention to Adopt, Trust, and Likeability, significantly outperforming both extremes. Personality alignment further enhanced outcomes, with Extraversion and Emotional Stability emerging as the most influential traits. Cluster analysis identified three distinct compatibility profiles, with \"Well-Aligned\" users reporting substantially positive perceptions. These findings demonstrate that personality expression and strategic trait alignment constitute optimal design targets for CA personality, offering design implications as LLM-based CAs become increasingly prevalent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, CHI 2026 conference paper (article no. 371)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:34Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalendu","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Nirmalendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeo Wei","familyName":"Jie","name":"Jie, Yeo Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Abdullah","name":"Abdullah, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjan","familyName":"Satapathy","name":"Satapathy, Ranjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Cambria","name":"Cambria, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka Wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond I'm Sorry, I Can't: Dissecting Large Language Model Refusal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T02:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T07:46:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:31:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T03:29:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:21:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Refusal on harmful prompts is a key safety behaviour in instruction-tuned large language models (LLMs), yet the internal causes of this behaviour remain poorly understood. We study two public instruction-tuned models, Gemma-2-2B-IT and LLaMA-3.1-8B-IT, using sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on residual-stream activations. Given a harmful prompt, we search the SAE latent space for feature sets whose ablation flips the model from refusal to compliance, demonstrating causal influence and creating a jailbreak. Our search proceeds in three stages: (1) Refusal Direction: find a refusal-mediating direction and collect SAE features near that direction; (2) Greedy Filtering: prune to a minimal set; and (3) Interaction Discovery: fit a factorization machine (FM) that captures nonlinear interactions among the remaining active features and the minimal set. This pipeline yields a broad set of jailbreak-critical features, offering insight into the mechanistic basis of refusal. Moreover, we find evidence of redundant features that remain dormant unless earlier features are suppressed. Our findings highlight the potential for fine-grained auditing and targeted intervention in safety behaviours by manipulating the interpretable latent space."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-29T02:31:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09893","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09893","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09893","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanbit","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Hanbit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaki","familyName":"Murooka","name":"Murooka, Masaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomohiro","familyName":"Motoda","name":"Motoda, Tomohiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryoichi","familyName":"Nakajo","name":"Nakajo, Ryoichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukiyasu","familyName":"Domae","name":"Domae, Yukiyasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Augmented Robot Trajectory: Efficient Imitation Learning via Safe Self-augmentation with Demonstrator-annotated Precision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T23:10:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T07:12:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:30:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imitation learning is a promising paradigm for training robot agents; however, standard approaches typically require substantial data acquisition -- via numerous demonstrations or random exploration -- to ensure reliable performance. Although exploration reduces human effort, it lacks safety guarantees and often results in frequent collisions -- particularly in clearance-limited tasks (e.g., peg-in-hole) -- thereby, necessitating manual environmental resets and imposing additional human burden. This study proposes Self-Augmented Robot Trajectory (SART), a framework that enables policy learning from a single human demonstration, while safely expanding the dataset through autonomous augmentation. SART consists of two stages: (1) human teaching only once, where a single demonstration is provided and precision boundaries -- represented as spheres around key waypoints -- are annotated, followed by one environment reset; (2) robot self-augmentation, where the robot generates diverse, collision-free trajectories within these boundaries and reconnects to the original demonstration. This design improves the data collection efficiency by minimizing human effort while ensuring safety. Extensive evaluations in simulation and real-world manipulation tasks show that SART achieves substantially higher success rates than policies trained solely on human-collected demonstrations. Video results available at https://sites.google.com/view/sart-il ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 10 figures, Advanced Robotics accepted 2026.02.03"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09893","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:20:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junqiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Chuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Meta-Learning Reinforcement Learning for Crypto-Return Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:20:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T10:15:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:06:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting cryptocurrency returns is notoriously difficult: price movements are driven by a fast-shifting blend of on-chain activity, news flow, and social sentiment, while labeled training data are scarce and expensive. In this paper, we present Meta-RL-Crypto, a unified transformer-based architecture that unifies meta-learning and reinforcement learning (RL) to create a fully self-improving trading agent. Starting from a vanilla instruction-tuned LLM, the agent iteratively alternates between three roles-actor, judge, and meta-judge-in a closed-loop architecture. This learning process requires no additional human supervision. It can leverage multimodal market inputs and internal preference feedback. The agent in the system continuously refines both the trading policy and evaluation criteria. Experiments across diverse market regimes demonstrate that Meta-RL-Crypto shows good performance on the technical indicators of the real market and outperforming other LLM-based baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:38:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maël","familyName":"Jullien","name":"Jullien, Maël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Valentino","name":"Valentino, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Freitas","name":"Freitas, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compartmentalised Agentic Reasoning for Clinical NLI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T13:14:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:35:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T11:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models can produce fluent judgments for clinical natural language inference, yet they frequently fail when the decision requires the correct inferential schema rather than surface matching. We introduce CARENLI, a compartmentalised agentic framework that routes each premise-statement pair to a reasoning family and then applies a specialised solver with explicit verification and targeted refinement. We evaluate on an expanded CTNLI benchmark of 200 instances spanning four reasoning families: Causal Attribution, Compositional Grounding, Epistemic Verification, and Risk State Abstraction. Across four contemporary backbone models, CARENLI improves mean accuracy from about 23% with direct prompting to about 57%, a gain of roughly 34 points, with the largest benefits on structurally demanding reasoning types. These results support compartmentalisation plus verification as a practical route to more reliable and auditable clinical inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yikuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiazun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suifeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Suifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weipeng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Weipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaorui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DB3 Team's Solution For Meta KDD Cup' 25"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-12T08:27:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T05:54:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the db3 team's winning solution for the Meta CRAG-MM Challenge 2025 at KDD Cup'25. Addressing the challenge's unique multi-modal, multi-turn question answering benchmark (CRAG-MM), we developed a comprehensive framework that integrates tailored retrieval pipelines for different tasks with a unified LLM-tuning approach for hallucination control. Our solution features (1) domain-specific retrieval pipelines handling image-indexed knowledge graphs, web sources, and multi-turn conversations; and (2) advanced refusal training using SFT, DPO, and RL. The system achieved 2nd place in Task 1, 2nd place in Task 2, and 1st place in Task 3, securing the grand prize for excellence in ego-centric queries through superior handling of first-person perspective challenges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:09Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:25:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09810","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09810","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09810","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnieszka","familyName":"Mensfelt","name":"Mensfelt, Agnieszka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Tena","familyName":"Cucala","name":"Cucala, David Tena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago","familyName":"Franco","name":"Franco, Santiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angeliki","familyName":"Koutsoukou-Argyraki","name":"Koutsoukou-Argyraki, Angeliki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vince","familyName":"Trencsenyi","name":"Trencsenyi, Vince","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostas","familyName":"Stathis","name":"Stathis, Kostas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards a Common Framework for Autoformalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T19:28:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T15:27:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:55:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T16:43:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:44:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoformalization has emerged as a term referring to the automation of formalization - specifically, the formalization of mathematics using interactive theorem provers (proof assistants). Its rapid development has been driven by progress in deep learning, especially large language models (LLMs). More recently, the term has expanded beyond mathematics to describe the broader task of translating informal input into formal logical representations. At the same time, a growing body of research explores using LLMs to translate informal language into formal representations for reasoning, planning, and knowledge representation - often without explicitly referring to this process as autoformalization. As a result, despite addressing similar tasks, the largely independent development of these research areas has limited opportunities for shared methodologies, benchmarks, and theoretical frameworks that could accelerate progress. The goal of this paper is to review - explicit or implicit - instances of what can be considered autoformalization and to propose a unified framework, encouraging cross-pollination between different fields to advance the development of next generation AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at NeLaMKRR@KR, 2025 (arXiv:2511.09575). A shorter version of this work will appear in the Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09810","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:20:09Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:20:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:49:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09711","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09711","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09711","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aya E.","familyName":"Fouda","name":"Fouda, Aya E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrahamn A.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Abdelrahamn A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radwa J.","familyName":"Hanafy","name":"Hanafy, Radwa J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed E.","familyName":"Fouda","name":"Fouda, Mohammed E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PsychiatryBench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T20:57:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-23T15:40:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) offer significant potential in enhancing psychiatric practice, from improving diagnostic accuracy to streamlining clinical documentation and therapeutic support. However, existing evaluation resources heavily rely on small clinical interview corpora, social media posts, or synthetic dialogues, which limits their clinical validity and fails to capture the full complexity of diagnostic reasoning. In this work, we introduce PsychiatryBench, a rigorously curated benchmark grounded exclusively in authoritative, expert-validated psychiatric textbooks and casebooks. PsychiatryBench comprises eleven distinct question-answering tasks ranging from diagnostic reasoning and treatment planning to longitudinal follow-up, management planning, clinical approach, sequential case analysis, and multiple-choice/extended matching formats totaling 5,188 expert-annotated items. {\\color{red}We evaluate a diverse set of frontier LLMs (including Google Gemini, DeepSeek, Sonnet 4.5, and GPT 5) alongside leading open-source medical models such as MedGemma using both conventional metrics and an \"LLM-as-judge\" similarity scoring framework. Our results reveal substantial gaps in clinical consistency and safety, particularly in multi-turn follow-up and management tasks, underscoring the need for specialized model tuning and more robust evaluation paradigms. PsychiatryBench offers a modular, extensible platform for benchmarking and improving LLM performance in mental health applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09711","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:50Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:49:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10266","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10266","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10266","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenfang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingting","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Tingting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daling","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Daling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SignMouth: Leveraging Mouthing Cues for Sign Language Translation by Multimodal Contrastive Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T14:08:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:37:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T22:32:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:14:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sign language translation (SLT) aims to translate natural language from sign language videos, serving as a vital bridge for inclusive communication. While recent advances leverage powerful visual backbones and large language models, most approaches mainly focus on manual signals (hand gestures) and tend to overlook non-manual cues like mouthing. In fact, mouthing conveys essential linguistic information in sign languages and plays a crucial role in disambiguating visually similar signs. In this paper, we propose SignClip, a novel framework to improve the accuracy of sign language translation. It fuses manual and non-manual cues, specifically spatial gesture and lip movement features. Besides, SignClip introduces a hierarchical contrastive learning framework with multi-level alignment objectives, ensuring semantic consistency across sign-lip and visual-text modalities. Extensive experiments on two benchmark datasets, PHOENIX14T and How2Sign, demonstrate the superiority of our approach. For example, on PHOENIX14T, in the Gloss-free setting, SignClip surpasses the previous state-of-the-art model SpaMo, improving BLEU-4 from 24.32 to 24.71, and ROUGE from 46.57 to 48.38."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10266","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:30:57Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:30:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:17:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninad","familyName":"Bhat","name":"Bhat, Ninad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kieran","familyName":"Browne","name":"Browne, Kieran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pip","familyName":"Bingemann","name":"Bingemann, Pip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Creativity Benchmark: A benchmark for marketing creativity for large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T04:44:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T23:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T01:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Creativity Benchmark, an evaluation framework for large language models (LLMs) in marketing creativity. The benchmark covers 100 brands (12 categories) and three prompt types (Insights, Ideas, Wild Ideas). Human pairwise preferences from 678 practising creatives over 11,012 anonymised comparisons, analysed with Bradley-Terry models, show tightly clustered performance with no model dominating across brands or prompt types: the top-bottom spread is $Δθ\\approx 0.45$, which implies a head-to-head win probability of $0.61$; the highest-rated model beats the lowest only about $61\\%$ of the time. We also analyse model diversity using cosine distances to capture intra- and inter-model variation and sensitivity to prompt reframing. Comparing three LLM-as-judge setups with human rankings reveals weak, inconsistent correlations and judge-specific biases, underscoring that automated judges cannot substitute for human evaluation. Conventional creativity tests also transfer only partially to brand-constrained tasks. Overall, the results highlight the need for expert human evaluation and diversity-aware workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 Pages, 14 figures. Fixed typos"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:38Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:40:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09942","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09942","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09942","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajia","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiajia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Secure and Explainable Smart Contract Generation with Security-Aware Group Relative Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T03:14:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T04:04:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:51:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Smart contracts automate the management of high-value assets, where vulnerabilities can lead to catastrophic financial losses. This challenge is amplified in Large Language Models (LLMs) by two interconnected failures: they operate as unauditable \"black boxes\" lacking a transparent reasoning process, and consequently, generate code riddled with critical security vulnerabilities. To address both issues, we propose SmartCoder-R1 (based on Qwen2.5-Coder-7B), a novel framework for secure and explainable smart contract generation. It begins with Continual Pre-training (CPT) to specialize the model. We then apply Long Chain-of-Thought Supervised Fine-Tuning (L-CoT SFT) on 7,998 expert-validated reasoning-and-code samples to train the model to emulate human security analysis. Finally, to directly mitigate vulnerabilities, we employ Security-Aware Group Relative Policy Optimization (S-GRPO), a reinforcement learning phase that refines the generation policy by optimizing a weighted reward signal for compilation success, security compliance, and format correctness. Evaluated against 17 baselines on a benchmark of 756 real-world functions, SmartCoder-R1 establishes a new state of the art, achieving top performance across five key metrics: a ComPass of 87.70%, a VulRate of 8.60%, a SafeAval of 80.16%, a FuncRate of 53.84%, and a FullRate of 50.53%. This FullRate marks a 45.79% relative improvement over the strongest baseline, DeepSeek-R1. Crucially, its generated reasoning also excels in human evaluations, achieving high-quality ratings for Functionality (82.7%), Security (85.3%), and Clarity (90.7%)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09942","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:17Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:20:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hailong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renhuo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Renhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guanjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAMA: A General Anonymizing Multi-Agent System for Privacy Preservation Enhanced by Domain Rules and Disproof Mechanism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T07:22:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:21:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T22:46:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs), LLM-based agents exhibit exceptional abilities in understanding and generating natural language, enabling human-like collaboration and information transmission in LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems (MAS). High-performance LLMs are often hosted on web servers in public cloud environments. When tasks involve private data, MAS cannot securely utilize these LLMs without implementing the agentic privacy-preserving mechanism. To address this challenge, we propose a General Anonymizing Multi-Agent System (GAMA), which divides the agents' workspace into private and public spaces, ensuring privacy through a structured anonymization mechanism. In the private space, agents handle sensitive data, while in the public web space, only anonymized data is utilized. GAMA incorporates two key modules to mitigate semantic loss caused by anonymization: Domain-Rule-based Knowledge Enhancement (DRKE) and Disproof-based Logic Enhancement (DLE). We evaluate GAMA on two general question-answering datasets, a public privacy leakage benchmark, and two customized question-answering datasets related to privacy. The results demonstrate that GAMA outperforms existing baselines on the evaluated datasets in terms of both task accuracy and privacy preservation metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:25:07Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:25:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:47:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxun","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Jianxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianfu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas Jing","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Nicholas Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Population-Aligned Persona Generation for LLM-based Social Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T10:43:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:29:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T09:04:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled human-like social simulations at unprecedented scale and fidelity, offering new opportunities for computational social science. A key challenge, however, is the construction of persona sets that authentically represent the diversity and distribution of real-world populations. Most existing LLM-based social simulation studies focus primarily on designing agentic frameworks and simulation environments, often overlooking the complexities of persona generation and the potential biases introduced by unrepresentative persona sets. In this paper, we propose a systematic framework for synthesizing high-quality, population-aligned persona sets for LLM-driven social simulation. Our approach begins by leveraging LLMs to generate narrative personas from long-term social media data, followed by rigorous quality assessment to filter out low-fidelity profiles. We then apply importance sampling to achieve global alignment with reference psychometric distributions, such as the Big Five personality traits. To address the needs of specific simulation contexts, we further introduce a task-specific module that adapts the globally aligned persona set to targeted subpopulations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method significantly reduces population-level bias and enables accurate, flexible social simulation for a wide range of research and policy applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:46:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alva","familyName":"West","name":"West, Alva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Abduct, Act, Predict: Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:51:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T14:45:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Failure attribution in multi-agent systems -- pinpointing the exact step where a decisive error occurs -- is a critical yet unsolved challenge. Current methods treat this as a pattern recognition task over long conversation logs, leading to critically low step-level accuracy (below 17\\%), which renders them impractical for debugging complex systems. Their core weakness is a fundamental inability to perform robust counterfactual reasoning: to determine if correcting a single action would have actually averted the task failure. To bridge this \\emph{counterfactual inference gap}, we introduce Abduct-Act-Predict (A2P) Scaffolding, a novel agent framework that transforms failure attribution from pattern recognition into a structured causal inference task. A2P explicitly guides a large language model through a formal three-step reasoning process within a single inference pass: (1) Abduction, to infer the hidden root causes behind an agent's actions; (2) Action, to define a minimal corrective intervention; and (3) Prediction, to simulate the subsequent trajectory and verify if the intervention resolves the failure. This structured approach leverages the holistic context of the entire conversation while imposing a rigorous causal logic on the model's analysis. Our extensive experiments on the Who\\\u0026amp;When benchmark demonstrate its efficacy. On the Algorithm-Generated dataset, A2P achieves 47.46\\% step-level accuracy, a 2.85$\\times$ improvement over the 16.67\\% of the baseline. On the more complex Hand-Crafted dataset, it achieves 29.31\\% step accuracy, a 2.43$\\times$ improvement over the baseline's 12.07\\%. By reframing the problem through a causal lens, A2P Scaffolding provides a robust, verifiable, and significantly more accurate solution for automated failure attribution. Ours code are released at https://github.com/ResearAI/A2P."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09716","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09716","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09716","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DongXiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, DongXiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengtao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Tengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VStyle: A Benchmark for Voice Style Adaptation with Spoken Instructions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T14:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T02:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spoken language models (SLMs) have emerged as a unified paradigm for speech understanding and generation, enabling natural human machine interaction. However, while most progress has focused on semantic accuracy and instruction following, the ability of SLMs to adapt their speaking style based on spoken instructions has received limited attention. We introduce Voice Style Adaptation (VSA), a new task that examines whether SLMs can modify their speaking style, such as timbre, prosody, or persona following natural language spoken commands. To study this task, we present VStyle, a bilingual (Chinese \u0026amp; English) benchmark covering four categories of speech generation: acoustic attributes, natural language instruction, role play, and implicit empathy. We also introduce the Large Audio Language Model as a Judge (LALM as a Judge) framework, which progressively evaluates outputs along textual faithfulness, style adherence, and naturalness, ensuring reproducible and objective assessment. Experiments on commercial systems and open source SLMs demonstrate that current models face clear limitations in controllable style adaptation, highlighting both the novelty and challenge of this task. By releasing VStyle and its evaluation toolkit, we aim to provide the community with a foundation for advancing human centered spoken interaction. The dataset and code are publicly available at \\href{https://junzhan2000.github.io/VStyle.github.io/}{project's homepage}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09716","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:57Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:52:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talha","familyName":"Tahir","name":"Tahir, Talha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Thinking Therapist: Training Large Language Models to Deliver Acceptance and Commitment Therapy using Supervised Fine-Tuning and Odds Ratio Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T02:30:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T21:31:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:39:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is a third-wave cognitive behavioral therapy with emerging evidence of efficacy in several psychiatric conditions. This study investigates the impact of post-training methodology and explicit reasoning on the ability of a small open-weight large language model (LLM) to deliver ACT. Using synthetic ACT transcripts generated by Mistral-Large, we trained Llama-3.2-3b-Instruct with two distinct approaches, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and odds ratio policy optimization (ORPO), each with and without an explicit chain-of-thought (COT) reasoning step. Performance was evaluated by comparing these four post-trained variants against the base Instruct model. These models were benchmarked in simulated therapy sessions, with performance quantitatively assessed on the ACT Fidelity Measure (ACT-FM) and the Therapist Empathy Scale (TES) by an LLM judge that had been fine-tuned on human evaluations. Our findings demonstrate that the ORPO-trained models significantly outperformed both their SFT and Instruct counterparts on ACT fidelity ($χ^2(5) = 185.15, p \u0026lt; .001$) and therapeutic empathy ($χ^2(5) = 140.37, p \u0026lt; .001$). The effect of COT was conditional as it provided a significant benefit to SFT models, improving ACT-FM scores by an average of 2.68 points ($p \u0026lt; .001$), while offering no discernible advantage to the superior ORPO or instruct-tuned variants. We posit that the superiority of ORPO stems from its ability to learn the therapeutic `process' over imitating `content,' a key aspect of ACT, while COT acts as a necessary scaffold for models trained only via imitation. This study establishes that preference-aligned policy optimization can effectively instill ACT competencies in small LLMs, and that the utility of explicit reasoning is highly dependent on the underlying training paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:51Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:49:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamir","familyName":"Shazman","name":"Shazman, Tamir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idan","familyName":"Lev-Yehudi","name":"Lev-Yehudi, Idan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ron","familyName":"Benchetit","name":"Benchetit, Ron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vadim","familyName":"Indelman","name":"Indelman, Vadim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online Robust Planning under Model Uncertainty: A Sample-Based Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T11:41:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:31:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T11:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online planning in Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) enables agents to make sequential decisions by simulating future trajectories from the current state, making it well-suited for large-scale or dynamic environments. Sample-based methods such as Sparse Sampling and Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) are widely adopted for their ability to approximate optimal actions using a generative model. However, in practical settings, the generative model is often learned from limited data, introducing approximation errors that can degrade performance or lead to unsafe behaviors. To address these challenges, Robust MDPs (RMDPs) offer a principled framework for planning under model uncertainty, yet existing approaches are typically computationally intensive and not suited for real-time use. In this work, we introduce Robust Sparse Sampling (RSS), the first online planning algorithm for RMDPs with finite-sample theoretical performance guarantees. Unlike Sparse Sampling, which estimates the nominal value function, RSS computes a robust value function by leveraging the efficiency and theoretical properties of Sample Average Approximation (SAA), enabling tractable robust policy computation in online settings. RSS is applicable to infinite or continuous state spaces, and its sample and computational complexities are independent of the state space size. We provide theoretical performance guarantees and empirically show that RSS outperforms standard Sparse Sampling in environments with uncertain dynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:32Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:47:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirill","familyName":"Vasilevski","name":"Vasilevski, Kirill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie M.","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinjia","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pinjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed E.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Ahmed E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SWE-Effi: Re-Evaluating Software AI Agent System Effectiveness Under Resource Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:04:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:06:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T19:31:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:06:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of large language models (LLMs) and code agents has demonstrated significant potential to assist software engineering (SWE) tasks, such as autonomous issue resolution and feature addition. Existing AI for software engineering leaderboards (e.g., SWE-bench) focus solely on solution accuracy, ignoring the crucial factor of effectiveness in a resource-constrained world. This is a universal problem that also exists beyond software engineering tasks: any AI system should be more than correct - it must also be cost-effective. To address this gap, we introduce SWE-Effi, a set of new metrics to re-evaluate AI systems in terms of holistic effectiveness scores. We define effectiveness as the balance between the accuracy of outcome (e.g., issue resolve rate) and the resources consumed (e.g., token and time). In this paper, we specifically focus on the software engineering scenario by re-ranking popular AI systems for issue resolution on a subset of the SWE-bench benchmark using our new multi-dimensional metrics. We found that AI system's effectiveness depends not just on the scaffold itself, but on how well it integrates with the base model, which is key to achieving strong performance in a resource-efficient manner. We also identified systematic challenges such as the \"token snowball\" effect and, more significantly, a pattern of \"expensive failures\". In these cases, agents consume excessive resources while stuck on unsolvable tasks - an issue that not only limits practical deployment but also drives up the cost of failed rollouts during RL training. Lastly, we observed a clear trade-off between effectiveness under the token budget and effectiveness under the time budget, which plays a crucial role in managing project budgets and enabling scalable reinforcement learning, where fast responses are essential."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:10Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:44:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bastián","familyName":"González-Bustamante","name":"González-Bustamante, Bastián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nando","familyName":"Verelst","name":"Verelst, Nando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carla","familyName":"Cisternas","name":"Cisternas, Carla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emulating Public Opinion: A Proof-of-Concept of AI-Generated Synthetic Survey Responses for the Chilean Case"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T50 (Primary) 91F10 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:43:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.17077752","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) offer promising avenues for methodological and applied innovations in survey research by using synthetic respondents to emulate human answers and behaviour, potentially mitigating measurement and representation errors. However, the extent to which LLMs recover aggregate item distributions remains uncertain and downstream applications risk reproducing social stereotypes and biases inherited from training data. We evaluate the reliability of LLM-generated synthetic survey responses against ground-truth human responses from a Chilean public opinion probabilistic survey. Specifically, we benchmark 128 prompt-model-question triplets, generating 189,696 synthetic profiles, and pool performance metrics (i.e., accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score) in a meta-analysis across 128 question-subsample pairs to test for biases along key sociodemographic dimensions. The evaluation spans OpenAI's GPT family and o-series reasoning models, as well as Llama and Qwen checkpoints. Three results stand out. First, synthetic responses achieve excellent performance on trust items (F1-score and accuracy \u0026gt; 0.90). Second, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini and Llama 4 Maverick perform comparably on this task. Third, synthetic-human alignment is highest among respondents aged 45-59. Overall, LLM-based synthetic samples approximate responses from a probabilistic sample, though with substantial item-level heterogeneity. Capturing the full nuance of public opinion remains challenging and requires careful calibration and additional distributional tests to ensure algorithmic fidelity and reduce errors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Working paper: 18 pages, 4 tables, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:36Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:43:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jianxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengqi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Wo","name":"Wo, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DECAMP: Towards Scene-Consistent Multi-Agent Motion Prediction with Disentangled Context-Aware Pre-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:29:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T06:22:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory prediction is a critical component of autonomous driving, essential for ensuring both safety and efficiency on the road. However, traditional approaches often struggle with the scarcity of labeled data and exhibit suboptimal performance in multi-agent prediction scenarios. To address these challenges, we introduce a disentangled context-aware pre-training framework for multi-agent motion prediction, named DECAMP. Unlike existing methods that entangle representation learning with pretext tasks, our framework decouples behavior pattern learning from latent feature reconstruction, prioritizing interpretable dynamics and thereby enhancing scene representation for downstream prediction. Additionally, our framework incorporates context-aware representation learning alongside collaborative spatial-motion pretext tasks, which enables joint optimization of structural and intentional reasoning while capturing the underlying dynamic intentions. Our experiments on the Argoverse 2 benchmark showcase the superior performance of our method, and the results attained underscore its effectiveness in multi-agent motion forecasting. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first context autoencoder framework for multi-agent motion forecasting in autonomous driving. The code and models will be made publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:45:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Caufield","name":"Caufield, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satrajit","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Satrajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sek Wong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Sek Wong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jillian","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Jillian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Sheffield","name":"Sheffield, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavesh","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Bhavesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Clark","name":"Clark, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monica C.","familyName":"Munoz-Torres","name":"Munoz-Torres, Monica C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Standards in the Preparation of Biomedical Research Metadata: A Bridge2AI Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Other Quantitative Biology (q-bio.OT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:38:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T20:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:08:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI-readiness describes the degree to which data may be optimally and ethically used for subsequent AI and Machine Learning (AI/ML) methods, where those methods may involve some combination of model training, data classification, and ethical, explainable prediction. The Bridge2AI consortium has defined the particular criteria a biomedical dataset may possess to render it AI-ready: in brief, a dataset's readiness is related to its FAIRness, provenance, degree of characterization, explainability, sustainability, and computability, in addition to its accompaniment with documentation about ethical data practices.\n To ensure AI-readiness and to clarify data structure and relationships within Bridge2AI's Grand Challenges (GCs), particular types of metadata are necessary. The GCs within the Bridge2AI initiative include four data-generating projects focusing on generating AI/ML-ready datasets to tackle complex biomedical and behavioral research problems. These projects develop standardized, multimodal data, tools, and training resources to support AI integration, while addressing ethical data practices. Examples include using voice as a biomarker, building interpretable genomic tools, modeling disease trajectories with diverse multimodal data, and mapping cellular and molecular health indicators across the human body.\n This report assesses the state of metadata creation and standardization in the Bridge2AI GCs, provides guidelines where required, and identifies gaps and areas for improvement across the program. New projects, including those outside the Bridge2AI consortium, would benefit from what we have learned about creating metadata as part of efforts to promote AI readiness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:52Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:41:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10345","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10345","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10345","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Pantazopoulos","name":"Pantazopoulos, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eda B.","familyName":"Özyiğit","name":"Özyiğit, Eda B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Understanding Visual Grounding in Visual Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:33:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:41:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T08:46:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:10:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual grounding refers to the ability of a model to identify a region within some visual input that matches a textual description. Consequently, a model equipped with visual grounding capabilities can target a wide range of applications in various domains, including referring expression comprehension, answering questions pertinent to fine-grained details in images or videos, caption visual context by explicitly referring to entities, as well as low and high-level control in simulated and real environments. In this survey paper, we review representative works across the key areas of research on modern general-purpose vision language models (VLMs). We first outline the importance of grounding in VLMs, then delineate the core components of the contemporary paradigm for developing grounded models, and examine their practical applications, including benchmarks and evaluation metrics for grounded multimodal generation. We also discuss the multifaceted interrelations among visual grounding, multimodal chain-of-thought, and reasoning in VLMs. Finally, we analyse the challenges inherent to visual grounding and suggest promising directions for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10345","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:49Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Giovannini","name":"Giovannini, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Gemelli","name":"Gemelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Coppini","name":"Coppini, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Marinai","name":"Marinai, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Reliable and Interpretable Document Question Answering via VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T10:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:29:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T02:38:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:59:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown strong capabilities in document understanding, particularly in identifying and extracting textual information from complex documents. Despite this, accurately localizing answers within documents remains a major challenge, limiting both interpretability and real-world applicability. To address this, we introduce DocExplainerV0, a plug-and-play bounding-box prediction module that decouples answer generation from spatial localization. This design makes it applicable to existing VLMs, including proprietary systems where fine-tuning is not feasible. Through systematic evaluation, we provide quantitative insights into the gap between textual accuracy and spatial grounding, showing that correct answers often lack reliable localization. Our standardized framework highlights these shortcomings and establishes a benchmark for future research toward more interpretable and robust document information extraction VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:45Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grant Michael","familyName":"Boquet","name":"Boquet, Grant Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoGPT-RAG Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.7; H.4; H.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T08:29:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T01:00:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GeoGPT is an open large language model system built to advance research in the geosciences. To enhance its domain-specific capabilities, we integrated Retrieval Augmented Generation(RAG), which augments model outputs with relevant information retrieved from an external knowledge source. GeoGPT uses RAG to draw from the GeoGPT Library, a specialized corpus curated for geoscientific content, enabling it to generate accurate, context-specific answers. Users can also create personalized knowledge bases by uploading their own publication lists, allowing GeoGPT to retrieve and respond using user-provided materials. To further improve retrieval quality and domain alignment, we fine-tuned both the embedding model and a ranking model that scores retrieved passages by relevance to the query. These enhancements optimize RAG for geoscience applications and significantly improve the system's ability to deliver precise and trustworthy outputs. GeoGPT reflects a strong commitment to open science through its emphasis on collaboration, transparency, and community driven development. As part of this commitment, we have open-sourced two core RAG components-GeoEmbedding and GeoReranker-to support geoscientists, researchers, and professionals worldwide with powerful, accessible AI tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 10 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:16Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cameron","familyName":"Reid","name":"Reid, Cameron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wael","familyName":"Hafez","name":"Hafez, Wael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Nazeri","name":"Nazeri, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mutual Information Tracks Policy Coherence in Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:24:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) agents deployed in real-world environments face degradation from sensor faults, actuator wear, and environmental shifts, yet lack intrinsic mechanisms to detect and diagnose these failures. We present an information-theoretic framework that reveals both the fundamental dynamics of RL and provides practical methods for diagnosing deployment-time anomalies. Through analysis of state-action mutual information patterns in a robotic control task, we first demonstrate that successful learning exhibits characteristic information signatures: mutual information between states and actions steadily increases from 0.84 to 2.83 bits (238% growth) despite growing state entropy, indicating that agents develop increasingly selective attention to task-relevant patterns. Intriguingly, states, actions and next states joint mutual information, MI(S,A;S'), follows an inverted U-curve, peaking during early learning before declining as the agent specializes suggesting a transition from broad exploration to efficient exploitation. More immediately actionable, we show that information metrics can differentially diagnose system failures: observation-space, i.e., states noise (sensor faults) produces broad collapses across all information channels with pronounced drops in state-action coupling, while action-space noise (actuator faults) selectively disrupts action-outcome predictability while preserving state-action relationships. This differential diagnostic capability demonstrated through controlled perturbation experiments enables precise fault localization without architectural modifications or performance degradation. By establishing information patterns as both signatures of learning and diagnostic for system health, we provide the foundation for adaptive RL systems capable of autonomous fault detection and policy adjustment based on information-theoretic principles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:38Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10344","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10344","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10344","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexi","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuexi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicha C.","familyName":"Dvornek","name":"Dvornek, Nicha C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAM: Geometry-Guided Local Alignment for Multi-View VLP in Mammography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:33:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:41:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mammography screening is an essential tool for early detection of breast cancer. The speed and accuracy of mammography interpretation have the potential to be improved with deep learning methods. However, the development of a foundation visual language model (VLM) is hindered by limited data and domain differences between natural and medical images. Existing mammography VLMs, adapted from natural images, often ignore domain-specific characteristics, such as multi-view relationships in mammography. Unlike radiologists who analyze both views together to process ipsilateral correspondence, current methods treat them as independent images or do not properly model the multi-view correspondence learning, losing critical geometric context and resulting in suboptimal prediction. We propose GLAM: Global and Local Alignment for Multi-view mammography for VLM pretraining using geometry guidance. By leveraging the prior knowledge about the multi-view imaging process of mammograms, our model learns local cross-view alignments and fine-grained local features through joint global and local, visual-visual, and visual-language contrastive learning. Pretrained on EMBED [14], one of the largest open mammography datasets, our model outperforms baselines across multiple datasets under different settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10344","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10326","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10326","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10326","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Lesnik","name":"Lesnik, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Schäfer","name":"Schäfer, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"State Algebra for Propositional Logic"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"03G27 (Primary) 68W30, 68T27 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:05:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:40:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents State Algebra, a novel framework designed to represent and manipulate propositional logic using algebraic methods. The framework is structured as a hierarchy of three representations: Set, Coordinate, and Row Decomposition. These representations anchor the system in well-known semantics while facilitating the computation using a powerful algebraic engine. A key aspect of State Algebra is its flexibility in representation. We show that although the default reduction of a state vector is not canonical, a unique canonical form can be obtained by applying a fixed variable order during the reduction process. This highlights a trade-off: by foregoing guaranteed canonicity, the framework gains increased flexibility, potentially leading to more compact representations of certain classes of problems. We explore how this framework provides tools to articulate both search-based and knowledge compilation algorithms and discuss its natural extension to probabilistic logic and Weighted Model Counting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10326","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:23Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:32:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eoin","familyName":"O'Doherty","name":"O'Doherty, Eoin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Weinrauch","name":"Weinrauch, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Talone","name":"Talone, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uri","familyName":"Klempner","name":"Klempner, Uri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Morality of Probability: How Implicit Moral Biases in LLMs May Shape the Future of Human-AI Symbiosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T14:37:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is advancing at a pace that raises urgent questions about how to align machine decision-making with human moral values. This working paper investigates how leading AI systems prioritize moral outcomes and what this reveals about the prospects for human-AI symbiosis. We address two central questions: (1) What moral values do state-of-the-art large language models (LLMs) implicitly favour when confronted with dilemmas? (2) How do differences in model architecture, cultural origin, and explainability affect these moral preferences? To explore these questions, we conduct a quantitative experiment with six LLMs, ranking and scoring outcomes across 18 dilemmas representing five moral frameworks. Our findings uncover strikingly consistent value biases. Across all models, Care and Virtue values outcomes were rated most moral, while libertarian choices were consistently penalized. Reasoning-enabled models exhibited greater sensitivity to context and provided richer explanations, whereas non-reasoning models produced more uniform but opaque judgments. This research makes three contributions: (i) Empirically, it delivers a large-scale comparison of moral reasoning across culturally distinct LLMs; (ii) Theoretically, it links probabilistic model behaviour with underlying value encodings; (iii) Practically, it highlights the need for explainability and cultural awareness as critical design principles to guide AI toward a transparent, aligned, and symbiotic future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:31:41Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:31:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:31:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iason","familyName":"Gabriel","name":"Gabriel, Iason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geoff","familyName":"Keeling","name":"Keeling, Geoff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arianna","familyName":"Manzini","name":"Manzini, Arianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"We Need a New Ethics for a World of AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T14:29:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:39:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/d41586-025-02454-5","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of capable AI agents raises fresh questions about safety, human-machine relationships and social coordination. We argue for greater engagement by scientists, scholars, engineers and policymakers with the implications of a world increasingly populated by AI agents. We explore key challenges that must be addressed to ensure that interactions between humans and agents, and among agents themselves, remain broadly beneficial."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, no figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:31:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:31:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:31:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Czech Technical University in Prague","Czech Institute of Informatics, Robotics and Cybernetics, Czech Technical University in Prague"],"givenName":"David","familyName":"Zahrádka","name":"Zahrádka, David","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Czech Technical University in Prague"],"givenName":"Denisa","familyName":"Mužíková","name":"Mužíková, Denisa","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Czech Institute of Informatics, Robotics and Cybernetics, Czech Technical University in Prague"],"givenName":"David","familyName":"Woller","name":"Woller, David","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Czech Institute of Informatics, Robotics and Cybernetics, Czech Technical University in Prague"],"givenName":"Miroslav","familyName":"Kulich","name":"Kulich, Miroslav","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Faculty of Mathematics and Physics, Charles University"],"givenName":"Jiří","familyName":"Švancara","name":"Švancara, Jiří","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Faculty of Mathematics and Physics, Charles University"],"givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Barták","name":"Barták, Roman","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Holistic Architecture for Monitoring and Optimization of Robust Multi-Agent Path Finding Plan Execution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T14:23:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The goal of Multi-Agent Path Finding (MAPF) is to find a set of paths for a fleet of agents moving in a shared environment such that the agents reach their goals without colliding with each other. In practice, some of the robots executing the plan may get delayed, which can introduce collision risk. Although robust execution methods are used to ensure safety even in the presence of delays, the delays may still have a significant impact on the duration of the execution. At some point, the accumulated delays may become significant enough that instead of continuing with the execution of the original plan, even if it was optimal, there may now exist an alternate plan which will lead to a shorter execution. However, the problem is how to decide when to search for the alternate plan, since it is a costly procedure. In this paper, we propose a holistic architecture for robust execution of MAPF plans, its monitoring and optimization. We exploit a robust execution method called Action Dependency Graph to maintain an estimate of the expected execution duration during the plan's execution. This estimate is used to predict the potential that finding an alternate plan would lead to shorter execution. We empirically evaluate the architecture in experiments in a real-time simulator which we designed to mimic our real-life demonstrator of an autonomous warehouse robotic fleet."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:31:23Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:31:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:31:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10249","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10249","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10249","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna Abi","familyName":"Akl","name":"Akl, Hanna Abi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating Language Model Capabilities to Represent and Process Formal Knowledge: A Preliminary Study to Assist Ontology Engineering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T13:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:36:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Language Models (LMs) have failed to mask their shortcomings particularly in the domain of reasoning. This limitation impacts several tasks, most notably those involving ontology engineering. As part of a PhD research, we investigate the consequences of incorporating formal methods on the performance of Small Language Models (SLMs) on reasoning tasks. Specifically, we aim to orient our work toward using SLMs to bootstrap ontology construction and set up a series of preliminary experiments to determine the impact of expressing logical problems with different grammars on the performance of SLMs on a predefined reasoning task. Our findings show that it is possible to substitute Natural Language (NL) with a more compact logical language while maintaining a strong performance on reasoning tasks and hope to use these results to further refine the role of SLMs in ontology engineering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted for the International Joint Conference on Rules and Reasoning (RuleML+RR) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10249","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:30:33Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:30:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:30:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Petković","name":"Petković, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vlado","familyName":"Menkovski","name":"Menkovski, Vlado","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofía","familyName":"Calero","name":"Calero, Sofía","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Fully Automated Molecular Simulations: Multi-Agent Framework for Simulation Setup and Force Field Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:34:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated characterization of porous materials has the potential to accelerate materials discovery, but it remains limited by the complexity of simulation setup and force field selection. We propose a multi-agent framework in which LLM-based agents can autonomously understand a characterization task, plan appropriate simulations, assemble relevant force fields, execute them and interpret their results to guide subsequent steps. As a first step toward this vision, we present a multi-agent system for literature-informed force field extraction and automated RASPA simulation setup. Initial evaluations demonstrate high correctness and reproducibility, highlighting this approach's potential to enable fully autonomous, scalable materials characterization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:39Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:29:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shengqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SI-FACT: Mitigating Knowledge Conflict via Self-Improving Faithfulness-Aware Contrastive Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:56:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models often generate unfaithful responses in knowledge intensive tasks due to knowledge conflict,that is,a preference for relying on internal parametric knowledge rather than the provided context.To address this issue,we propose a novel self improving framework,Self Improving Faithfulness Aware Contrastive Tuning.The framework uses a self instruct mechanism that allows the base LLM to automatically generate high quality,structured contrastive learning data,including anchor samples,semantically equivalent positive samples,and negative samples simulating unfaithful scenarios.This approach significantly reduces the cost of manual annotation.Subsequently,contrastive learning is applied to train the model,enabling it to pull faithful responses closer and push unfaithful responses farther apart in the representation space.Experiments on knowledge conflict evaluation benchmarks ECARE KRE and COSE KRE show that the SI FACT model based on Llama3 8B Instruct improves the Contextual Recall Rate by 6.2% over the best baseline method,while significantly reducing dependence on internal memory.The results indicate that SI FACT provides strong effectiveness and high data efficiency in enhancing the contextual faithfulness of LLMs,offering a practical pathway toward building more proactive and trustworthy language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:36Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:29:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenad","familyName":"Tomasev","name":"Tomasev, Nenad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matija","familyName":"Franklin","name":"Franklin, Matija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel Z.","familyName":"Leibo","name":"Leibo, Joel Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Jacobs","name":"Jacobs, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William A.","familyName":"Cunningham","name":"Cunningham, William A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iason","familyName":"Gabriel","name":"Gabriel, Iason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Osindero","name":"Osindero, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Virtual Agent Economies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T11:20:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:30:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid adoption of autonomous AI agents is giving rise to a new economic layer where agents transact and coordinate at scales and speeds beyond direct human oversight. We propose the \"sandbox economy\" as a framework for analyzing this emergent system, characterizing it along two key dimensions: its origins (emergent vs. intentional) and its degree of separateness from the established human economy (permeable vs. impermeable). Our current trajectory points toward a spontaneous emergence of a vast and highly permeable AI agent economy, presenting us with opportunities for an unprecedented degree of coordination as well as significant challenges, including systemic economic risk and exacerbated inequality. Here we discuss a number of possible design choices that may lead to safely steerable AI agent markets. In particular, we consider auction mechanisms for fair resource allocation and preference resolution, the design of AI \"mission economies\" to coordinate around achieving collective goals, and socio-technical infrastructure needed to ensure trust, safety, and accountability. By doing this, we argue for the proactive design of steerable agent markets to ensure the coming technological shift aligns with humanity's long-term collective flourishing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:11Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:28:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10099","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10099","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10099","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu","familyName":"Apsan","name":"Apsan, Radu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Stoico","name":"Stoico, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michel","familyName":"Albonico","name":"Albonico, Michel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rudra","familyName":"Dhar","name":"Dhar, Rudra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Vaidhyanathan","name":"Vaidhyanathan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivano","familyName":"Malavolta","name":"Malavolta, Ivano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Energy-Efficient Code via Large-Language Models -- Where are we now?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:49:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Context. The rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to their widespread adoption in development pipelines. Goal. We empirically assess the energy efficiency of Python code generated by LLMs against human-written code and code developed by a Green software expert. Method. We test 363 solutions to 9 coding problems from the EvoEval benchmark using 6 widespread LLMs with 4 prompting techniques, and comparing them to human-developed solutions. Energy consumption is measured on three different hardware platforms: a server, a PC, and a Raspberry Pi for a total of ~881h (36.7 days). Results. Human solutions are 16% more energy-efficient on the server and 3% on the Raspberry Pi, while LLMs outperform human developers by 25% on the PC. Prompting does not consistently lead to energy savings, where the most energy-efficient prompts vary by hardware platform. The code developed by a Green software expert is consistently more energy-efficient by at least 17% to 30% against all LLMs on all hardware platforms. Conclusions. Even though LLMs exhibit relatively good code generation capabilities, no LLM-generated code was more energy-efficient than that of an experienced Green software developer, suggesting that as of today there is still a great need of human expertise for developing energy-efficient Python code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10099","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:03Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:27:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Litong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengcheng","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Mengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qingyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Mathematical Reasoning Embedded in Aerial Vehicle Imagery: Benchmarking, Analysis, and Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T08:46:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mathematical reasoning is critical for tasks such as precise distance and area computations, trajectory estimations, and spatial analysis in unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) based remote sensing, yet current vision-language models (VLMs) have not been adequately tested in this domain. To address this gap, we introduce AVI-Math, the first benchmark to rigorously evaluate multimodal mathematical reasoning in aerial vehicle imagery, moving beyond simple counting tasks to include domain-specific knowledge in areas such as geometry, logic, and algebra. The dataset comprises 3,773 high-quality vehicle-related questions captured from UAV views, covering 6 mathematical subjects and 20 topics. The data, collected at varying altitudes and from multiple UAV angles, reflects real-world UAV scenarios, ensuring the diversity and complexity of the constructed mathematical problems. In this paper, we benchmark 14 prominent VLMs through a comprehensive evaluation and demonstrate that, despite their success on previous multimodal benchmarks, these models struggle with the reasoning tasks in AVI-Math. Our detailed analysis highlights significant limitations in the mathematical reasoning capabilities of current VLMs and suggests avenues for future research. Furthermore, we explore the use of Chain-of-Thought prompting and fine-tuning techniques, which show promise in addressing the reasoning challenges in AVI-Math. Our findings not only expose the limitations of VLMs in mathematical reasoning but also offer valuable insights for advancing UAV-based trustworthy VLMs in real-world applications. The code, and datasets will be released at https://github.com/VisionXLab/avi-math"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 16 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:06Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:26:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Mojsiejuk","name":"Mojsiejuk, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sławomir","familyName":"Ziętek","name":"Ziętek, Sławomir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Witold","familyName":"Skowroński","name":"Skowroński, Witold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement learning for spin torque oscillator tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T08:41:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:24:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We address the problem of automatic synchronisation of the spintronic oscillator (STO) by means of reinforcement learning (RL). A numerical solution of the macrospin Landau-Lifschitz-Gilbert-Slonczewski equation is used to simulate the STO and we train the two types of RL agents to synchronise with a target frequency within a fixed number of steps. We explore modifications to this base task and show an improvement in both convergence and energy efficiency of the synchronisation that can be easily achieved in the simulated environment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 figures, 6 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:04Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:26:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10054","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10054","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10054","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hailong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxian","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Mingxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guanjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XAgents: A Unified Framework for Multi-Agent Cooperation via IF-THEN Rules and Multipolar Task Processing Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T08:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:24:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has significantly enhanced the capabilities of Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) in supporting humans with complex, real-world tasks. However, MAS still face challenges in effective task planning when handling highly complex tasks with uncertainty, often resulting in misleading or incorrect outputs that hinder task execution. To address this, we propose XAgents, a unified multi-agent cooperative framework built on a multipolar task processing graph and IF-THEN rules. XAgents uses the multipolar task processing graph to enable dynamic task planning and handle task uncertainty. During subtask processing, it integrates domain-specific IF-THEN rules to constrain agent behaviors, while global rules enhance inter-agent collaboration. We evaluate the performance of XAgents across three distinct datasets, demonstrating that it consistently surpasses state-of-the-art single-agent and multi-agent approaches in both knowledge-typed and logic-typed question-answering tasks. The codes for XAgents are available at: https://github.com/AGI-FHBC/XAgents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10054","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:25:59Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:26:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ponhvoan","familyName":"Srey","name":"Srey, Ponhvoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh Tuan","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Anh Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Hallucination Detection by Inspecting Reasoning Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T06:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised hallucination detection aims to identify hallucinated content generated by large language models (LLMs) without relying on labeled data. While unsupervised methods have gained popularity by eliminating labor-intensive human annotations, they frequently rely on proxy signals unrelated to factual correctness. This misalignment biases detection probes toward superficial or non-truth-related aspects, limiting generalizability across datasets and scenarios. To overcome these limitations, we propose IRIS, an unsupervised hallucination detection framework, leveraging internal representations intrinsic to factual correctness. IRIS prompts the LLM to carefully verify the truthfulness of a given statement, and obtain its contextualized embedding as informative features for training. Meanwhile, the uncertainty of each response is considered a soft pseudolabel for truthfulness. Experimental results demonstrate that IRIS consistently outperforms existing unsupervised methods. Our approach is fully unsupervised, computationally low cost, and works well even with few training data, making it suitable for real-time detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stav","familyName":"Armoni-Friedmann","name":"Armoni-Friedmann, Stav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hana","familyName":"Chockler","name":"Chockler, Hana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David A.","familyName":"Kelly","name":"Kelly, David A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluation of Black-Box XAI Approaches for Predictors of Values of Boolean Formulae"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T05:52:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:17:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating explainable AI (XAI) approaches is a challenging task in general, due to the subjectivity of explanations. In this paper, we focus on tabular data and the specific use case of AI models predicting the values of Boolean functions. We extend the previous work in this domain by proposing a formal and precise measure of importance of variables based on actual causality, and we evaluate state-of-the-art XAI tools against this measure. We also present a novel XAI tool B-ReX, based on the existing tool ReX, and demonstrate that it is superior to other black-box XAI tools on a large-scale benchmark. Specifically, B-ReX achieves a Jensen-Shannon divergence of 0.072 $\\pm$ 0.012 on random 10-valued Boolean formulae"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECAI-EXCD Workshop, 8 pages, 2 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:24:13Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:24:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09972","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09972","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09972","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Narimani","name":"Narimani, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Pourreza","name":"Pourreza, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Moghimi","name":"Moghimi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Mesgaran","name":"Mesgaran, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parastoo","familyName":"Farajpoor","name":"Farajpoor, Parastoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Jafarbiglu","name":"Jafarbiglu, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Drone-Based Multispectral Imaging and Deep Learning for Timely Detection of Branched Broomrape in Tomato Farms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T05:16:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1117/12.3021219.","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study addresses the escalating threat of branched broomrape (Phelipanche ramosa) to California's tomato industry, which supplies over 90 percent of U.S. processing tomatoes. The parasite's largely underground life cycle makes early detection difficult, while conventional chemical controls are costly, environmentally harmful, and often ineffective. To address this, we combined drone-based multispectral imagery with Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) deep learning networks, using the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) to handle class imbalance. Research was conducted on a known broomrape-infested tomato farm in Woodland, Yolo County, CA, across five key growth stages determined by growing degree days (GDD). Multispectral images were processed to isolate tomato canopy reflectance. At 897 GDD, broomrape could be detected with 79.09 percent overall accuracy and 70.36 percent recall without integrating later stages. Incorporating sequential growth stages with LSTM improved detection substantially. The best-performing scenario, which integrated all growth stages with SMOTE augmentation, achieved 88.37 percent overall accuracy and 95.37 percent recall. These results demonstrate the strong potential of temporal multispectral analysis and LSTM networks for early broomrape detection. While further real-world data collection is needed for practical deployment, this study shows that UAV-based multispectral sensing coupled with deep learning could provide a powerful precision agriculture tool to reduce losses and improve sustainability in tomato production."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Author-accepted version (no publisher header/footer). 10 pages + presentation. Published in Proceedings of SPIE Defense + Commercial Sensing 2024, Vol. 13053, Paper 1305304. Event: National Harbor, Maryland, USA. Official version: https://doi.org/10.1117/12.3021219"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09972","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:59Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:24:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Moein","familyName":"Abtahi","name":"Abtahi, Seyed Moein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akramul","familyName":"Azim","name":"Azim, Akramul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Securing LLM-Generated Embedded Firmware through AI Agent-Driven Validation and Patching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T05:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) show promise in generating firmware for embedded systems, but often introduce security flaws and fail to meet real-time performance constraints. This paper proposes a three-phase methodology that combines LLM-based firmware generation with automated security validation and iterative refinement in a virtualized environment. Using structured prompts, models like GPT-4 generate firmware for networking and control tasks, deployed on FreeRTOS via QEMU. These implementations are tested using fuzzing, static analysis, and runtime monitoring to detect vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows (CWE-120), race conditions (CWE-362), and denial-of-service threats (CWE-400). Specialized AI agents for Threat Detection, Performance Optimization, and Compliance Verification collaborate to improve detection and remediation. Identified issues are categorized using CWE, then used to prompt targeted LLM-generated patches in an iterative loop. Experiments show a 92.4\\% Vulnerability Remediation Rate (37.3\\% improvement), 95.8\\% Threat Model Compliance, and 0.87 Security Coverage Index. Real-time metrics include 8.6ms worst-case execution time and 195μs jitter. This process enhances firmware security and performance while contributing an open-source dataset for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:56Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:23:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitian","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanli","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Nanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyong","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Tianyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Meet Legal Artificial Intelligence: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T05:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have significantly advanced the development of Legal Artificial Intelligence (Legal AI) in recent years, enhancing the efficiency and accuracy of legal tasks. To advance research and applications of LLM-based approaches in legal domain, this paper provides a comprehensive review of 16 legal LLMs series and 47 LLM-based frameworks for legal tasks, and also gather 15 benchmarks and 29 datasets to evaluate different legal capabilities. Additionally, we analyse the challenges and discuss future directions for LLM-based approaches in the legal domain. We hope this paper provides a systematic introduction for beginners and encourages future research in this field. Resources are available at https://github.com/ZhitianHou/LLMs4LegalAI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Moein","familyName":"Abtahi","name":"Abtahi, Seyed Moein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akramul","familyName":"Azim","name":"Azim, Akramul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WALL: A Web Application for Automated Quality Assurance using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T01:43:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:11:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As software projects become increasingly complex, the volume and variety of issues in code files have grown substantially. Addressing this challenge requires efficient issue detection, resolution, and evaluation tools. This paper presents WALL, a web application that integrates SonarQube and large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-3.5 Turbo and GPT-4o to automate these tasks. WALL comprises three modules: an issue extraction tool, code issues reviser, and code comparison tool. Together, they enable a seamless pipeline for detecting software issues, generating automated code revisions, and evaluating the accuracy of revisions. Our experiments, conducted on 563 files with over 7,599 issues, demonstrate WALL's effectiveness in reducing human effort while maintaining high-quality revisions. Results show that employing a hybrid approach of cost-effective and advanced LLMs can significantly lower costs and improve revision rates. Future work aims to enhance WALL's capabilities by integrating open-source LLMs and eliminating human intervention, paving the way for fully automated code quality management."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:44Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:22:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woong","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Woong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renan","familyName":"Souza","name":"Souza, Renan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Rosendo","name":"Rosendo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frédéric","familyName":"Suter","name":"Suter, Frédéric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Feiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasanna","familyName":"Balaprakash","name":"Balaprakash, Prasanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael Ferreira","familyName":"da Silva","name":"da Silva, Rafael Ferreira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The (R)evolution of Scientific Workflows in the Agentic AI Era: Towards Autonomous Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T01:14:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:11:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern scientific discovery increasingly requires coordinating distributed facilities and heterogeneous resources, forcing researchers to act as manual workflow coordinators rather than scientists. Advances in AI leading to AI agents show exciting new opportunities that can accelerate scientific discovery by providing intelligence as a component in the ecosystem. However, it is unclear how this new capability would materialize and integrate in the real world. To address this, we propose a conceptual framework where workflows evolve along two dimensions which are intelligence (from static to intelligent) and composition (from single to swarm) to chart an evolutionary path from current workflow management systems to fully autonomous, distributed scientific laboratories. With these trajectories in mind, we present an architectural blueprint that can help the community take the next steps towards harnessing the opportunities in autonomous science with the potential for 100x discovery acceleration and transformational scientific workflows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:40Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:22:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09911","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09911","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09911","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barkin","familyName":"Buyukcakir","name":"Buyukcakir, Barkin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jannick","familyName":"De Tobel","name":"De Tobel, Jannick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Thevissen","name":"Thevissen, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dirk","familyName":"Vandermeulen","name":"Vandermeulen, Dirk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Claes","name":"Claes, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Autoencoder and Vision Transformer-based Interpretability Analysis of the Differences in Automated Staging of Second and Third Molars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T07 (Primary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T00:54:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The practical adoption of deep learning in high-stakes forensic applications, such as dental age estimation, is often limited by the 'black box' nature of the models. This study introduces a framework designed to enhance both performance and transparency in this context. We use a notable performance disparity in the automated staging of mandibular second (tooth 37) and third (tooth 38) molars as a case study. The proposed framework, which combines a convolutional autoencoder (AE) with a Vision Transformer (ViT), improves classification accuracy for both teeth over a baseline ViT, increasing from 0.712 to 0.815 for tooth 37 and from 0.462 to 0.543 for tooth 38. Beyond improving performance, the framework provides multi-faceted diagnostic insights. Analysis of the AE's latent space metrics and image reconstructions indicates that the remaining performance gap is data-centric, suggesting high intra-class morphological variability in the tooth 38 dataset is a primary limiting factor. This work highlights the insufficiency of relying on a single mode of interpretability, such as attention maps, which can appear anatomically plausible yet fail to identify underlying data issues. By offering a methodology that both enhances accuracy and provides evidence for why a model may be uncertain, this framework serves as a more robust tool to support expert decision-making in forensic age estimation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 11 figures, Scientific Reports"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09911","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:34Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:22:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Fetsch","name":"Fetsch, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iurii","familyName":"Savvateev","name":"Savvateev, Iurii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Racem Ben","familyName":"Romdhane","name":"Romdhane, Racem Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Wiedmann","name":"Wiedmann, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artemiy","familyName":"Dimov","name":"Dimov, Artemiy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Durkalec","name":"Durkalec, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Teichmann","name":"Teichmann, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Zinsstag","name":"Zinsstag, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Koutsoumanis","name":"Koutsoumanis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreja","familyName":"Rajkovic","name":"Rajkovic, Andreja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Tonolla","name":"Tonolla, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monika","familyName":"Ehling-Schulz","name":"Ehling-Schulz, Monika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Filter","name":"Filter, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia","familyName":"Johler","name":"Johler, Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tackling One Health Risks: How Large Language Models are leveraged for Risk Negotiation and Consensus-building"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:10:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Key global challenges of our times are characterized by complex interdependencies and can only be effectively addressed through an integrated, participatory effort. Conventional risk analysis frameworks often reduce complexity to ensure manageability, creating silos that hinder comprehensive solutions. A fundamental shift towards holistic strategies is essential to enable effective negotiations between different sectors and to balance the competing interests of stakeholders. However, achieving this balance is often hindered by limited time, vast amounts of information, and the complexity of integrating diverse perspectives. This study presents an AI-assisted negotiation framework that incorporates large language models (LLMs) and AI-based autonomous agents into a negotiation-centered risk analysis workflow. The framework enables stakeholders to simulate negotiations, systematically model dynamics, anticipate compromises, and evaluate solution impacts. By leveraging LLMs' semantic analysis capabilities we could mitigate information overload and augment decision-making process under time constraints. Proof-of-concept implementations were conducted in two real-world scenarios: (i) prudent use of a biopesticide, and (ii) targeted wild animal population control. Our work demonstrates the potential of AI-assisted negotiation to address the current lack of tools for cross-sectoral engagement. Importantly, the solution's open source, web based design, suits for application by a broader audience with limited resources and enables users to tailor and develop it for their own needs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:27Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:22:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yago Romano","familyName":"Matinez","name":"Matinez, Yago Romano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Roberts","name":"Roberts, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs as Agentic Cooperative Players in Multiplayer UNO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:07:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs promise to assist humans -- not just by answering questions, but by offering useful guidance across a wide range of tasks. But how far does that assistance go? Can a large language model based agent actually help someone accomplish their goal as an active participant? We test this question by engaging an LLM in UNO, a turn-based card game, asking it not to win but instead help another player to do so. We built a tool that allows decoder-only LLMs to participate as agents within the RLCard game environment. These models receive full game-state information and respond using simple text prompts under two distinct prompting strategies. We evaluate models ranging from small (1B parameters) to large (70B parameters) and explore how model scale impacts performance. We find that while all models were able to successfully outperform a random baseline when playing UNO, few were able to significantly aid another player."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny Y.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jenny Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehul","familyName":"Damani","name":"Damani, Mehul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousef","familyName":"El-Kurdi","name":"El-Kurdi, Yousef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramon","familyName":"Astudillo","name":"Astudillo, Ramon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latency and Token-Aware Test-Time Compute"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference-time scaling has emerged as a powerful way to improve large language model (LLM) performance by generating multiple candidate responses and selecting among them. However, existing work on dynamic allocation for test-time compute typically considers only parallel generation methods such as best-of-N, overlooking incremental decoding methods like beam search, and has largely ignored latency, focusing only on token usage. We formulate inference-time scaling as a problem of dynamic compute allocation and method selection, where the system must decide which strategy to apply and how much compute to allocate on a per-query basis. Our framework explicitly incorporates both token cost and wall-clock latency, the latter being critical for user experience and particularly for agentic workflows where models must issue multiple queries efficiently. Experiments on reasoning benchmarks show that our approach consistently outperforms static strategies, achieving favorable accuracy-cost trade-offs while remaining practical for deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09848","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09848","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09848","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nana","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Nana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Norton","name":"Norton, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards an AI-based knowledge assistant for goat farmers based on Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T20:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being recognised as valuable knowledge communication tools in many industries. However, their application in livestock farming remains limited, being constrained by several factors not least the availability, diversity and complexity of knowledge sources. This study introduces an intelligent knowledge assistant system designed to support health management in farmed goats. Leveraging the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), two structured knowledge processing methods, table textualization and decision-tree textualization, were proposed to enhance large language models' (LLMs) understanding of heterogeneous data formats. Based on these methods, a domain-specific goat farming knowledge base was established to improve LLM's capacity for cross-scenario generalization. The knowledge base spans five key domains: Disease Prevention and Treatment, Nutrition Management, Rearing Management, Goat Milk Management, and Basic Farming Knowledge. Additionally, an online search module is integrated to enable real-time retrieval of up-to-date information. To evaluate system performance, six ablation experiments were conducted to examine the contribution of each component. The results demonstrated that heterogeneous knowledge fusion method achieved the best results, with mean accuracies of 87.90% on the validation set and 84.22% on the test set. Across the text-based, table-based, decision-tree based Q\u0026amp;A tasks, accuracy consistently exceeded 85%, validating the effectiveness of structured knowledge fusion within a modular design. Error analysis identified omission as the predominant error category, highlighting opportunities to further improve retrieval coverage and context integration. In conclusion, the results highlight the robustness and reliability of the proposed system for practical applications in goat farming."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09848","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:03Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09801","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09801","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09801","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brennen","familyName":"Hill","name":"Hill, Brennen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HEFT: A Coarse-to-Fine Hierarchy for Enhancing the Efficiency and Accuracy of Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; C.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T50, 68T05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T19:06:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:04:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The adaptation of large language models (LLMs) to specialized reasoning tasks is fundamentally constrained by computational resources. Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods have emerged as a powerful solution, yet the landscape of these techniques is diverse, with distinct methods operating in either the model's weight space or its representation space. This paper investigates the hypothesis that a synergistic combination of these paradigms can unlock superior performance and efficiency. We introduce HEFT (Hierarchical Efficient Fine-Tuning), a novel hierarchical adaptation strategy that composes two distinct PEFT methods in a coarse-to-fine manner: first, a broad, foundational adaptation in the weight space using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), followed by a precise, surgical refinement of internal activations using Representation Fine-Tuning (ReFT). We evaluate this approach by fine-tuning a Llama-2-7B model on the BoolQ benchmark, a challenging dataset for inferential reasoning. Our results reveal a profound synergistic effect. A model fine-tuned for only three epochs with our HEFT strategy achieves an accuracy of 85.17\\%, exceeding the performance of models trained for 20 epochs with either LoRA-only (85.05\\%) or ReFT-only (83.36\\%) methodologies. This work demonstrates that the thoughtful composition of PEFT methods is a potent algorithmic innovation, offering a more efficient and effective path toward advancing the reasoning capabilities of language models. By achieving superior results with a fraction of the computational budget, our findings present a principled approach to overcoming the obstacles inherent in adapting large-scale models for complex cognitive tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09801","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:19:56Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:19:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:19:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How well can LLMs provide planning feedback in grounded environments?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T18:51:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:03:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning to plan in grounded environments typically requires carefully designed reward functions or high-quality annotated demonstrations. Recent works show that pretrained foundation models, such as large language models (LLMs) and vision language models (VLMs), capture background knowledge helpful for planning, which reduces the amount of reward design and demonstrations needed for policy learning. We evaluate how well LLMs and VLMs provide feedback across symbolic, language, and continuous control environments. We consider prominent types of feedback for planning including binary feedback, preference feedback, action advising, goal advising, and delta action feedback. We also consider inference methods that impact feedback performance, including in-context learning, chain-of-thought, and access to environment dynamics. We find that foundation models can provide diverse high-quality feedback across domains. Moreover, larger and reasoning models consistently provide more accurate feedback, exhibit less bias, and benefit more from enhanced inference methods. Finally, feedback quality degrades for environments with complex dynamics or continuous state spaces and action spaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:19:41Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:19:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengze","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daxin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Daxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Cam-Tu","name":"Cam-Tu, Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAVa: Layer-wise KV Cache Eviction with Dynamic Budget Allocation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:48:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV Cache is commonly used to accelerate LLM inference with long contexts, yet its high memory demand drives the need for cache compression. Existing compression methods, however, are largely heuristic and lack dynamic budget allocation. To address this limitation, we introduce a unified framework for cache compression by minimizing information loss in Transformer residual streams. Building on it, we analyze the layer attention output loss and derive a new metric to compare cache entries across heads, enabling layer-wise compression with dynamic head budgets. Additionally, by contrasting cross-layer information, we also achieve dynamic layer budgets. LAVa is the first unified strategy for cache eviction and dynamic budget allocation that, unlike prior methods, does not rely on training or the combination of multiple strategies. Experiments with benchmarks (LongBench, Needle-In-A-Haystack, Ruler, and InfiniteBench) demonstrate its superiority. Moreover, our experiments reveal a new insight: dynamic layer budgets are crucial for generation tasks (e.g., code completion), while dynamic head budgets play a key role in extraction tasks (e.g., extractive QA). As a fully dynamic compression method, LAVa consistently maintains top performance across task types. Our code is available at https://github.com/MGDDestiny/Lava."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing-Yue Monica","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Xing-Yue Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron Archer","familyName":"Waterman","name":"Waterman, Aaron Archer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommaso","familyName":"Biancalani","name":"Biancalani, Tommaso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Richmond","name":"Richmond, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yogesh","familyName":"Pandit","name":"Pandit, Yogesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avtar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Avtar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russell","familyName":"Littman","name":"Littman, Russell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan-Christian","familyName":"Huetter","name":"Huetter, Jan-Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Ermakov","name":"Ermakov, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HypoGeneAgent: A Hypothesis Language Agent for Gene-Set Cluster Resolution Selection Using Perturb-seq Datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T22:25:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale single-cell and Perturb-seq investigations routinely involve clustering cells and subsequently annotating each cluster with Gene-Ontology (GO) terms to elucidate the underlying biological programs. However, both stages, resolution selection and functional annotation, are inherently subjective, relying on heuristics and expert curation. We present HYPOGENEAGENT, a large language model (LLM)-driven framework, transforming cluster annotation into a quantitatively optimizable task. Initially, an LLM functioning as a gene-set analyst analyzes the content of each gene program or perturbation module and generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses, accompanied by calibrated confidence scores. Subsequently, we embed every predicted description with a sentence-embedding model, compute pair-wise cosine similarities, and let the agent referee panel score (i) the internal consistency of the predictions, high average similarity within the same cluster, termed intra-cluster agreement (ii) their external distinctiveness, low similarity between clusters, termed inter-cluster separation. These two quantities are combined to produce an agent-derived resolution score, which is maximized when clusters exhibit simultaneous coherence and mutual exclusivity. When applied to a public K562 CRISPRi Perturb-seq dataset as a preliminary test, our Resolution Score selects clustering granularities that exhibit alignment with known pathway compared to classical metrics such silhouette score, modularity score for gene functional enrichment summary. These findings establish LLM agents as objective adjudicators of cluster resolution and functional annotation, thereby paving the way for fully automated, context-aware interpretation pipelines in single-cell multi-omics studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umut","familyName":"Eser","name":"Eser, Umut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yael","familyName":"Gozin","name":"Gozin, Yael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. Jay","familyName":"Stallons","name":"Stallons, L. Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ari","familyName":"Caroline","name":"Caroline, Ari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Preusse","name":"Preusse, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Rice","name":"Rice, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Robertson","name":"Robertson, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human-AI Collaboration Increases Efficiency in Regulatory Writing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:02:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Investigational New Drug (IND) application preparation is time-intensive and expertise-dependent, slowing early clinical development. Objective: To evaluate whether a large language model (LLM) platform (AutoIND) can reduce first-draft composition time while maintaining document quality in regulatory submissions. Methods: Drafting times for IND nonclinical written summaries (eCTD modules 2.6.2, 2.6.4, 2.6.6) generated by AutoIND were directly recorded. For comparison, manual drafting times for IND summaries previously cleared by the U.S. FDA were estimated from the experience of regulatory writers ($\\geq$6 years) and used as industry-standard benchmarks. Quality was assessed by a blinded regulatory writing assessor using seven pre-specified categories: correctness, completeness, conciseness, consistency, clarity, redundancy, and emphasis. Each sub-criterion was scored 0-3 and normalized to a percentage. A critical regulatory error was defined as any misrepresentation or omission likely to alter regulatory interpretation (e.g., incorrect NOAEL, omission of mandatory GLP dose-formulation analysis). Results: AutoIND reduced initial drafting time by $\\sim$97% (from $\\sim$100 h to 3.7 h for 18,870 pages/61 reports in IND-1; and to 2.6 h for 11,425 pages/58 reports in IND-2). Quality scores were 69.6\\% and 77.9\\% for IND-1 and IND-2. No critical regulatory errors were detected, but deficiencies in emphasis, conciseness, and clarity were noted. Conclusions: AutoIND can dramatically accelerate IND drafting, but expert regulatory writers remain essential to mature outputs to submission-ready quality. Systematic deficiencies identified provide a roadmap for targeted model improvements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:28Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09737","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09737","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09737","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klemen","familyName":"Kotar","name":"Kotar, Klemen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanhee","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wanhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Bear","name":"Bear, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Watrous","name":"Watrous, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khai Loong","familyName":"Aw","name":"Aw, Khai Loong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lilian Naing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lilian Naing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Stojanov","name":"Stojanov, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Feigelis","name":"Feigelis, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Thobani","name":"Thobani, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Durango","name":"Durango, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khaled","familyName":"Jedoui","name":"Jedoui, Khaled","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atlas","familyName":"Kazemian","name":"Kazemian, Atlas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Yamins","name":"Yamins, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"World Modeling with Probabilistic Structure Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:01:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Probabilistic Structure Integration (PSI), a system for learning richly controllable and flexibly promptable world models from data. PSI consists of a three-step cycle. The first step, Probabilistic prediction, involves building a probabilistic graphical model Psi of the data, in the form of a random-access autoregressive sequence model. Psi supports a complete set of learned conditional distributions describing the dependence of any variables in the data on any other set of variables. In step 2, Structure extraction, we show how to extract underlying low-dimensional properties in the data, corresponding to a diverse set of meaningful \"intermediate structures\", in a zero-shot fashion via causal inference on Psi. Step 3, Integration, completes the cycle by converting these structures into new token types that are then continually mixed back into the training diet as conditioning signals and prediction targets. Each such cycle augments the capabilities of Psi, both allowing it to model the underlying data better, and creating new control handles -- akin to an LLM-like universal prompting language. We train an instance of Psi on 1.4 trillion tokens of internet video data; we use it to perform a variety of useful video prediction and understanding inferences; we extract state-of-the-art optical flow, self-supervised depth and object segmentation; and we use these structures to support a full cycle of predictive improvements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09737","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09734","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09734","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09734","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Benfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiwei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaorui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCP-AgentBench: Evaluating Real-World Language Agent Performance with MCP-Mediated Tools"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T14:08:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is rapidly emerging as a pivotal open standard, designed to enhance agent-tool integration and interoperability, and is positioned to unlock a new era of powerful, interconnected, and genuinely utilitarian agentic AI. However, despite MCP's growing adoption, existing benchmarks often fail to capture real-world agent performance within this new paradigm, leading to a distorted perception of their true operational value and an inability to reliably differentiate proficiencies. To bridge this critical evaluation gap, we introduce MCP-AgentBench -- a comprehensive benchmark specifically engineered to rigorously assess language agent capabilities in MCP-mediated tool interactions. Core contributions of MCP-AgentBench include: the establishment of a robust MCP testbed comprising 33 operational servers with 188 distinct tools; the development of a benchmark featuring 600 systematically designed queries distributed across 6 distinct categories of varying interaction complexity; and the introduction of MCP-Eval, a novel outcome-oriented evaluation methodology prioritizing real-world task success. Through extensive empirical evaluation of leading language agents, we provide foundational insights. MCP-AgentBench aims to equip the research community with a standardized and reliable framework to build, validate, and advance agents capable of fully leveraging MCP's transformative benefits, thereby accelerating progress toward truly capable and interoperable AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09734","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:22Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09729","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09729","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09729","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerard","familyName":"Sant","name":"Sant, Gerard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Escolano","name":"Escolano, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Moryossef","name":"Moryossef, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rico","familyName":"Sennrich","name":"Sennrich, Rico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Ebling","name":"Ebling, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MultimodalHugs: Enabling Sign Language Processing in Hugging Face"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T11:14:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, sign language processing (SLP) has gained importance in the general field of Natural Language Processing. However, compared to research on spoken languages, SLP research is hindered by complex ad-hoc code, inadvertently leading to low reproducibility and unfair comparisons. Existing tools that are built for fast and reproducible experimentation, such as Hugging Face, are not flexible enough to seamlessly integrate sign language experiments. This view is confirmed by a survey we conducted among SLP researchers.\n To address these challenges, we introduce MultimodalHugs, a framework built on top of Hugging Face that enables more diverse data modalities and tasks, while inheriting the well-known advantages of the Hugging Face ecosystem. Even though sign languages are our primary focus, MultimodalHugs adds a layer of abstraction that makes it more widely applicable to other use cases that do not fit one of the standard templates of Hugging Face. We provide quantitative experiments to illustrate how MultimodalHugs can accommodate diverse modalities such as pose estimation data for sign languages, or pixel data for text characters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09729","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:15Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Wonyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeong","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Sujeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Juhyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiTTO-LLM: Framework for Discovering Topic-based Technology Opportunities via Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T09","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T05:47:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Technology opportunities are critical information that serve as a foundation for advancements in technology, industry, and innovation. This paper proposes a framework based on the temporal relationships between technologies to identify emerging technology opportunities. The proposed framework begins by extracting text from a patent dataset, followed by mapping text-based topics to discover inter-technology relationships. Technology opportunities are then identified by tracking changes in these topics over time. To enhance efficiency, the framework leverages a large language model to extract topics and employs a prompt for a chat-based language model to support the discovery of technology opportunities. The framework was evaluated using an artificial intelligence patent dataset provided by the United States Patent and Trademark Office. The experimental results suggest that artificial intelligence technology is evolving into forms that facilitate everyday accessibility. This approach demonstrates the potential of the proposed framework to identify future technology opportunities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingxin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dingxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal RAG Framework for Housing Damage Assessment: Collaborative Optimization of Image Encoding and Policy Vector Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T01:58:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"After natural disasters, accurate evaluations of damage to housing are important for insurance claims response and planning of resources. In this work, we introduce a novel multimodal retrieval-augmented generation (MM-RAG) framework. On top of classical RAG architecture, we further the framework to devise a two-branch multimodal encoder structure that the image branch employs a visual encoder composed of ResNet and Transformer to extract the characteristic of building damage after disaster, and the text branch harnesses a BERT retriever for the text vectorization of posts as well as insurance policies and for the construction of a retrievable restoration index. To impose cross-modal semantic alignment, the model integrates a cross-modal interaction module to bridge the semantic representation between image and text via multi-head attention. Meanwhile, in the generation module, the introduced modal attention gating mechanism dynamically controls the role of visual evidence and text prior information during generation. The entire framework takes end-to-end training, and combines the comparison loss, the retrieval loss and the generation loss to form multi-task optimization objectives, and achieves image understanding and policy matching in collaborative learning. The results demonstrate superior performance in retrieval accuracy and classification index on damage severity, where the Top-1 retrieval accuracy has been improved by 9.6%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:04Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naveen","familyName":"Lamba","name":"Lamba, Naveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanju","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Sanju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manas","familyName":"Gaur","name":"Gaur, Manas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating Symbolic Triggers of Hallucination in Gemma Models Across HaluEval and TruthfulQA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T05:50:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucination in Large Language Models (LLMs) is a well studied problem. However, the properties that make LLM intrinsically vulnerable to hallucinations have not been identified and studied. This research identifies and characterizes the key properties, allowing us to pinpoint vulnerabilities within the model's internal mechanisms. To solidify on these properties, we utilized two established datasets, HaluEval and TruthfulQA and convert their existing format of question answering into various other formats to narrow down these properties as the reason for the hallucinations. Our findings reveal that hallucination percentages across symbolic properties are notably high for Gemma-2-2B, averaging 79.0% across tasks and datasets. With increased model scale, hallucination drops to 73.6% for Gemma-2-9B and 63.9% for Gemma-2-27B, reflecting a 15 percentage point reduction overall. Although the hallucination rate decreases as the model size increases, a substantial amount of hallucination caused by symbolic properties still persists. This is especially evident for modifiers (ranging from 84.76% to 94.98%) and named entities (ranging from 83.87% to 93.96%) across all Gemma models and both datasets. These findings indicate that symbolic elements continue to confuse the models, pointing to a fundamental weakness in how these LLMs process such inputs--regardless of their scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09714","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09714","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09714","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serge Lionel","familyName":"Nikiema","name":"Nikiema, Serge Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albérick Euraste","familyName":"Djire","name":"Djire, Albérick Euraste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdoul Aziz","familyName":"Bonkoungou","name":"Bonkoungou, Abdoul Aziz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Micheline Bénédicte","familyName":"Moumoula","name":"Moumoula, Micheline Bénédicte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Samhi","name":"Samhi, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdoul Kader","familyName":"Kabore","name":"Kabore, Abdoul Kader","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacques","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Jacques","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tegawendé F.","familyName":"Bissyande","name":"Bissyande, Tegawendé F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Small Transformation Expose the Weakness of Semantic Similarity Measures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T11:00:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research examines how well different methods measure semantic similarity, which is important for various software engineering applications such as code search, API recommendations, automated code reviews, and refactoring tools. While large language models are increasingly used for these similarity assessments, questions remain about whether they truly understand semantic relationships or merely recognize surface patterns.\n The study tested 18 different similarity measurement approaches, including word-based methods, embedding techniques, LLM-based systems, and structure-aware algorithms. The researchers created a systematic testing framework that applies controlled changes to text and code to evaluate how well each method handles different types of semantic relationships.\n The results revealed significant issues with commonly used metrics. Some embedding-based methods incorrectly identified semantic opposites as similar up to 99.9 percent of the time, while certain transformer-based approaches occasionally rated opposite meanings as more similar than synonymous ones. The study found that embedding methods' poor performance often stemmed from how they calculate distances; switching from Euclidean distance to cosine similarity improved results by 24 to 66 percent. LLM-based approaches performed better at distinguishing semantic differences, producing low similarity scores (0.00 to 0.29) for genuinely different meanings, compared to embedding methods that incorrectly assigned high scores (0.82 to 0.99) to dissimilar content."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09714","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:54Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09713","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09713","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09713","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duolin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Duolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhehao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianzhen","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Lianzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjie","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HANRAG: Heuristic Accurate Noise-resistant Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Multi-hop Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T06:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach enhances question-answering systems and dialogue generation tasks by integrating information retrieval (IR) technologies with large language models (LLMs). This strategy, which retrieves information from external knowledge bases to bolster the response capabilities of generative models, has achieved certain successes. However, current RAG methods still face numerous challenges when dealing with multi-hop queries. For instance, some approaches overly rely on iterative retrieval, wasting too many retrieval steps on compound queries. Additionally, using the original complex query for retrieval may fail to capture content relevant to specific sub-queries, resulting in noisy retrieved content. If the noise is not managed, it can lead to the problem of noise accumulation. To address these issues, we introduce HANRAG, a novel heuristic-based framework designed to efficiently tackle problems of varying complexity. Driven by a powerful revelator, HANRAG routes queries, decomposes them into sub-queries, and filters noise from retrieved documents. This enhances the system's adaptability and noise resistance, making it highly capable of handling diverse queries. We compare the proposed framework against other leading industry methods across various benchmarks. The results demonstrate that our framework obtains superior performance in both single-hop and multi-hop question-answering tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09713","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09709","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09709","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09709","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Assisting Research Proposal Writing with Large Language Models: Evaluation and Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T10:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT are increasingly used in academic writing, yet issues such as incorrect or fabricated references raise ethical concerns. Moreover, current content quality evaluations often rely on subjective human judgment, which is labor-intensive and lacks objectivity, potentially compromising the consistency and reliability. In this study, to provide a quantitative evaluation and enhance research proposal writing capabilities of LLMs, we propose two key evaluation metrics--content quality and reference validity--and an iterative prompting method based on the scores derived from these two metrics. Our extensive experiments show that the proposed metrics provide an objective, quantitative framework for assessing ChatGPT's writing performance. Additionally, iterative prompting significantly enhances content quality while reducing reference inaccuracies and fabrications, addressing critical ethical challenges in academic contexts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09709","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:47Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camilo Chacón","familyName":"Sartori","name":"Sartori, Camilo Chacón","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martín Isla","familyName":"Pino","name":"Pino, Martín Isla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Pinacho-Davidson","name":"Pinacho-Davidson, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Blum","name":"Blum, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Instance-Driven Heuristic Bias In the Context of a Biased Random Key Genetic Algorithm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T21:46:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) within metaheuristics opens a novel path for solving complex combinatorial optimization problems. While most existing approaches leverage LLMs for code generation to create or refine specific heuristics, they often overlook the structural properties of individual problem instances. In this work, we introduce a novel framework that integrates LLMs with a Biased Random-Key Genetic Algorithm (BRKGA) to solve the NP-hard Longest Run Subsequence problem. Our approach extends the instance-driven heuristic bias paradigm by introducing a human-LLM collaborative process to co-design and implement a set of computationally efficient metrics. The LLM analyzes these instance-specific metrics to generate a tailored heuristic bias, which steers the BRKGA toward promising areas of the search space. We conduct a comprehensive experimental evaluation, including rigorous statistical tests, convergence and behavioral analyses, and targeted ablation studies, comparing our method against a standard BRKGA baseline across 1,050 generated instances of varying complexity. Results show that our top-performing hybrid, BRKGA+Llama-4-Maverick, achieves statistically significant improvements over the baseline, particularly on the most complex instances. Our findings confirm that leveraging an LLM to produce an a priori, instance-driven heuristic bias is a valuable approach for enhancing metaheuristics in complex optimization domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to a journal for review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taniya","familyName":"Gidatkar","name":"Gidatkar, Taniya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwaseun","familyName":"Ajao","name":"Ajao, Oluwaseun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Shardlow","name":"Shardlow, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differential Robustness in Transformer Language Models: Empirical Evaluation Under Adversarial Text Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T21:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study evaluates the resilience of large language models (LLMs) against adversarial attacks, specifically focusing on Flan-T5, BERT, and RoBERTa-Base. Using systematically designed adversarial tests through TextFooler and BERTAttack, we found significant variations in model robustness. RoBERTa-Base and FlanT5 demonstrated remarkable resilience, maintaining accuracy even when subjected to sophisticated attacks, with attack success rates of 0%. In contrast. BERT-Base showed considerable vulnerability, with TextFooler achieving a 93.75% success rate in reducing model accuracy from 48% to just 3%. Our research reveals that while certain LLMs have developed effective defensive mechanisms, these safeguards often require substantial computational resources. This study contributes to the understanding of LLM security by identifying existing strengths and weaknesses in current safeguarding approaches and proposes practical recommendations for developing more efficient and effective defensive strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 tables, to appear in proceedings of Recent Advances in Natural Language Processing (RANLP 2025) and ACL Anthology"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Pinhanez","name":"Pinhanez, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo","familyName":"Cavalin","name":"Cavalin, Paulo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassia","familyName":"Sanctos","name":"Sanctos, Cassia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Grave","name":"Grave, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yago","familyName":"Primerano","name":"Primerano, Yago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Non-Determinism of Small LLMs: Evidence of Low Answer Consistency in Repetition Trials of Standard Multiple-Choice Benchmarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T17:31:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work explores the consistency of small LLMs (2B-8B parameters) in answering multiple times the same question. We present a study on known, open-source LLMs responding to 10 repetitions of questions from the multiple-choice benchmarks MMLU-Redux and MedQA, considering different inference temperatures, small vs. medium models (50B-80B), finetuned vs. base models, and other parameters. We also look into the effects of requiring multi-trial answer consistency on accuracy and the trade-offs involved in deciding which model best provides both of them. To support those studies, we propose some new analytical and graphical tools. Results show that the number of questions which can be answered consistently vary considerably among models but are typically in the 50%-80% range for small models at low inference temperatures. Also, accuracy among consistent answers seems to reasonably correlate with overall accuracy. Results for medium-sized models seem to indicate much higher levels of answer consistency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Mazyaki","name":"Mazyaki, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Naghizadeh","name":"Naghizadeh, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samaneh Ranjkhah","familyName":"Zonouzaghi","name":"Zonouzaghi, Samaneh Ranjkhah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Setareh","name":"Setareh, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Temporal Preferences in Language Models for Long-Horizon Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T16:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study whether language models (LMs) exhibit future- versus present-oriented preferences in intertemporal choice and whether those preferences can be systematically manipulated. Using adapted human experimental protocols, we evaluate multiple LMs on time-tradeoff tasks and benchmark them against a sample of human decision makers. We introduce an operational metric, the Manipulability of Time Orientation (MTO), defined as the change in an LM's revealed time preference between future- and present-oriented prompts. In our tests, reasoning-focused models (e.g., DeepSeek-Reasoner and grok-3-mini) choose later options under future-oriented prompts but only partially personalize decisions across identities or geographies. Moreover, models that correctly reason about time orientation internalize a future orientation for themselves as AI decision makers. We discuss design implications for AI assistants that should align with heterogeneous, long-horizon goals and outline a research agenda on personalized contextual calibration and socially aware deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixiang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xubin","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Xubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengwei","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Shengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changting","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Changting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CTCC: A Robust and Stealthy Fingerprinting Framework for Large Language Models via Cross-Turn Contextual Correlation Backdoor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T05:59:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread deployment of large language models (LLMs) has intensified concerns around intellectual property (IP) protection, as model theft and unauthorized redistribution become increasingly feasible. To address this, model fingerprinting aims to embed verifiable ownership traces into LLMs. However, existing methods face inherent trade-offs between stealthness, robustness, and generalizability, being either detectable via distributional shifts, vulnerable to adversarial modifications, or easily invalidated once the fingerprint is revealed. In this work, we introduce CTCC, a novel rule-driven fingerprinting framework that encodes contextual correlations across multiple dialogue turns, such as counterfactual, rather than relying on token-level or single-turn triggers. CTCC enables fingerprint verification under black-box access while mitigating false positives and fingerprint leakage, supporting continuous construction under a shared semantic rule even if partial triggers are exposed. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM architectures demonstrate that CTCC consistently achieves stronger stealth and robustness than prior work. Our findings position CTCC as a reliable and practical solution for ownership verification in real-world LLM deployment scenarios. Our code and data are publicly available at ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP2025 MainConference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:39Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09690","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09690","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09690","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jianqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunnan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chunnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajat","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Rajat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baofen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Baofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caleb","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Caleb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangjie","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Liangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Powering Job Search at Scale: LLM-Enhanced Query Understanding in Job Matching Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-19T21:35:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3760913","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Query understanding is essential in modern relevance systems, where user queries are often short, ambiguous, and highly context-dependent. Traditional approaches often rely on multiple task-specific Named Entity Recognition models to extract structured facets as seen in job search applications. However, this fragmented architecture is brittle, expensive to maintain, and slow to adapt to evolving taxonomies and language patterns. In this paper, we introduce a unified query understanding framework powered by a Large Language Model (LLM), designed to address these limitations. Our approach jointly models the user query and contextual signals such as profile attributes to generate structured interpretations that drive more accurate and personalized recommendations. The framework improves relevance quality in online A/B testing while significantly reducing system complexity and operational overhead. The results demonstrate that our solution provides a scalable and adaptable foundation for query understanding in dynamic web applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CIKM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09690","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:21Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Thakur","name":"Thakur, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eshani","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Eshani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smruthi","familyName":"Mukund","name":"Mukund, Smruthi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personas within Parameters: Fine-Tuning Small Language Models with Low-Rank Adapters to Mimic User Behaviors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T22:14:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A long-standing challenge in developing accurate recommendation models is simulating user behavior, mainly due to the complex and stochastic nature of user interactions. Towards this, one promising line of work has been the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) for simulating user behavior. However, aligning these general-purpose large pre-trained models with user preferences necessitates: (i) effectively and continously parsing large-scale tabular user-item interaction data, (ii) overcoming pre-training-induced inductive biases to accurately learn user specific knowledge, and (iii) achieving the former two at scale for millions of users. While most previous works have focused on complex methods to prompt an LLM or fine-tune it on tabular interaction datasets, our approach shifts the focus to extracting robust textual user representations using a frozen LLM and simulating cost-effective, resource-efficient user agents powered by fine-tuned Small Language Models (SLMs). Further, we showcase a method for training multiple low-rank adapters for groups of users or \\textit{persona}, striking an optimal balance between scalability and performance of user behavior agents. Our experiments provide compelling empirical evidence of the efficacy of our methods, demonstrating that user agents developed using our approach have the potential to bridge the gap between offline metrics and real-world performance of recommender systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09688","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09688","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09688","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Atif","name":"Atif, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Garonne","name":"Garonne, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Lancon","name":"Lancon, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerome","familyName":"Lauret","name":"Lauret, Jerome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandr","familyName":"Prozorov","name":"Prozorov, Alexandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Vranovsky","name":"Vranovsky, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI-Powered Assistant for Long-Term Access to RHIC Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T15:16:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) at Brookhaven National Laboratory concludes 25 years of operation, preserving not only its vast data holdings ($\\sim$1 ExaByte) but also the embedded scientific knowledge becomes a critical priority. The RHIC Data and Analysis Preservation Plan (DAPP) introduces an AI-powered assistant system that provides natural language access to documentation, workflows, and software, with the aim of supporting reproducibility, education, and future discovery. Built upon Large Language Models using Retrieval-Augmented Generation and the Model Context Protocol, this assistant indexes structured and unstructured content from RHIC experiments and enables domain-adapted interaction. We report on the deployment, computational performance, ongoing multi-experiment integration, and architectural features designed for a sustainable and explainable long-term AI access. Our experience illustrates how modern AI/ML tools can transform the usability and discoverability of scientific legacy data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09688","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:18Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":84,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=subjects.subject%3A%28%22cs.AI%22+OR+%22cs.IR%22+OR+%22cs.MA%22%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+tool+OR+tools%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+tool+OR+tools%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-14T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=200\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}